{"data":[{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00536","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00536","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00536","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kung-Hsiang","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Kung-Hsiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoyi","familyName":"Qiu","name":"Qiu, Haoyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yutong","familyName":"Dai","name":"Dai, Yutong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Caiming","familyName":"Xiong","name":"Xiong, Caiming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chien-Sheng","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Chien-Sheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"GUI-KV: Efficient GUI Agents via KV Cache with Spatio-Temporal Awareness"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T05:37:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:30:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-10-07T23:23:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-09T00:19:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Graphical user interface (GUI) agents built on vision-language models have emerged as a promising approach to automate human-computer workflows. However, they also face the inefficiency challenge as they process long sequences of high-resolution screenshots and solving long-horizon tasks, making inference slow, costly and memory-bound. While key-value (KV) caching can mitigate this, storing the full cache is prohibitive for image-heavy contexts. Existing cache-compression methods are sub-optimal as they do not account for the spatial and temporal redundancy of GUIs. In this work, we first analyze attention patterns in GUI agent workloads and find that, unlike in natural images, attention sparsity is uniformly high across all transformer layers. This insight motivates a simple uniform budget allocation strategy, which we show empirically outperforms more complex layer-varying schemes. Building on this, we introduce GUI-KV, a plug-and-play KV cache compression method for GUI agents that requires no retraining. GUI-KV combines two novel techniques: (i) spatial saliency guidance, which augments attention scores with the L2 norm of hidden states to better preserve semantically important visual tokens, and (ii) temporal redundancy scoring, which projects previous frames' keys onto the current frame's key subspace to preferentially prune redundant history. Across standard GUI agent benchmarks and models, GUI-KV outperforms competitive KV compression baselines, closely matching full-cache accuracy at modest budgets. Notably, in a 5-screenshot setting on the AgentNetBench benchmark, GUI-KV reduces decoding FLOPs by 38.9% while increasing step accuracy by 4.1% over the full-cache baseline. These results demonstrate that exploiting GUI-specific redundancies enables efficient and reliable agent performance."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by TMLR"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00536","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T02:02:15Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:02:16Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-09T03:15:02Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00527","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00527","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00527","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Taeyun","familyName":"Woo","name":"Woo, Taeyun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinah","familyName":"Park","name":"Park, Jinah","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tae-Kyun","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Tae-Kyun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Cascaded Diffusion Framework for Probabilistic Coarse-to-Fine Hand Pose Estimation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T05:19:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:29:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-09-06T12:16:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-09T01:01:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Deterministic models for 3D hand pose reconstruction, whether single-staged or cascaded, struggle with pose ambiguities caused by self-occlusions and complex hand articulations. Existing cascaded approaches progressively refine pose predictions in a coarse-to-fine manner, but their deterministic nature prevents them from modeling pose uncertainty. Conversely, recent probabilistic methods capture pose distributions but are confined to single-stage estimation, often yielding inaccurate 3D reconstructions without refinement. To address these limitations, we propose a coarse-to-fine cascaded diffusion framework that combines probabilistic modeling with cascaded refinement. The first stage is a joint diffusion model that samples diverse 3D joint hypotheses, and the second stage is a Mesh Latent Diffusion Model (Mesh LDM) that reconstructs a 3D hand mesh conditioned on a joint sample. Our key idea is to use these diverse hypotheses as a training signal rather than as final outputs, so that the Mesh LDM learns distribution-aware joint-mesh relationships and becomes robust to the variation in coarse predictions. Extensive ablations validate the necessity of the cascaded design and the choice of latent-space diffusion. Experiments on FreiHAND, HO3Dv2, and DexYCB show that our method achieves state-of-the-art performance and remains stable under occlusion and pose ambiguity."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"18 pages, 6 figures, accepted to ECCV 2026 DexHAND workshops"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00527","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T02:02:02Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:02:03Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-09T05:54:19Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01028","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01028","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01028","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruqian","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Ruqian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yijiao","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yijiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Juan","familyName":"Shen","name":"Shen, Juan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhongyi","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Zhongyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Annie","familyName":"Qu","name":"Qu, Annie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Syntax-Guided Diffusion Language Models with User-Integrated Personalization"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Methodology (stat.ME)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T15:33:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T01:02:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-22T13:51:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-25T00:31:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models have made revolutionary progress in generating human-like text, yet their outputs often tend to be generic, exhibiting insufficient structural diversity, which limits personalized expression. Recent advances in diffusion models have opened new opportunities for improving language generation beyond the limitations of autoregressive paradigms. In this work, we propose a syntax-guided diffusion language model that integrates structural supervision and personalized conditioning to enhance text quality, diversity, and controllability. We introduce a cascaded framework that generates syntactic guidance before conditional text generation, and further generalize it to a novel noncascaded architecture for better alignment between structure and content. By incorporating syntactic information in the generating process, the proposed model better captures the lexical and structural characteristics of stylistic sentence construction. To enable fine-grained personalization, we develop a shared representation mechanism that facilitates information integration across users, supporting both faithful stylistic generation and generalizable zero-shot inference. Extensive experiments on multiple tasks demonstrate the superiority of our approach in fluency, diversity, and stylistic fidelity. Further qualitative analyses highlight its interpretability and flexibility in learning personalized patterns."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01028","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T02:14:48Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:14:49Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-25T03:50:05Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00506","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00506","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00506","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Naru","familyName":"Suzuki","name":"Suzuki, Naru","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Takehiko","familyName":"Ohkawa","name":"Ohkawa, Takehiko","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tatsuro","familyName":"Banno","name":"Banno, Tatsuro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jihyun","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Jihyun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ryosuke","familyName":"Furuta","name":"Furuta, Ryosuke","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yoichi","familyName":"Sato","name":"Sato, Yoichi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Affordance-Guided Diffusion Prior for 3D Hand Reconstruction"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T04:36:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:27:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-07-14T04:39:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-15T00:24:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-08T13:26:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-08-11T00:34:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"How can we reconstruct 3D hand poses when large portions of the hand are heavily occluded by itself or by objects? Humans often resolve such ambiguities by leveraging contextual knowledge -- such as affordances, where an object's shape and function suggest how the object is typically grasped. Inspired by this observation, we propose a generative prior for hand pose refinement guided by affordance-aware textual descriptions of hand-object interactions (HOI). Our method employs a diffusion-based generative model that learns the distribution of plausible hand poses conditioned on affordance descriptions, which are inferred from a large vision-language model (VLM). This enables the refinement of occluded regions into more accurate and functionally coherent hand poses. Extensive experiments on HOGraspNet, a 3D hand-affordance dataset with severe occlusions, demonstrate that our affordance-guided refinement significantly improves hand pose estimation over both recent regression methods and diffusion-based refinement lacking contextual reasoning."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to ECCV 2026. Project page: https://narusuzuki.github.io/projects/26-affhandgen/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00506","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T02:01:27Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:01:28Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-11T04:12:32Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00037","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00037","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00037","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianing","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Jianing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhenhong","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Zhenhong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chang","familyName":"Tu","name":"Tu, Chang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiyao","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Yiyao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiangqi","familyName":"Kong","name":"Kong, Xiangqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiqian","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Zhiqian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaming","familyName":"Ji","name":"Ji, Jiaming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuning","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Shuning","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuanpei","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yuanpei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kai","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Kai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qi","familyName":"Dou","name":"Dou, Qi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yaodong","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Yaodong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xianglong","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Xianglong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huijie","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Huijie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weifeng","familyName":"Lv","name":"Lv, Weifeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Simin","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Simin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"RobustVLA: On Robustness of Vision-Language-Action Model against Multi-Modal Perturbations"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-26T14:42:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:00:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-15T08:40:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-16T00:36:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-28T09:55:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10-31T00:52:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-02-24T07:21:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:28:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-07-19T04:43:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2026-07-21T00:48:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2026-07-27T06:20:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v6"},{"date":"2026-07-28T01:15:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v6"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"6","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In Vision-Language-Actionf(VLA) models, robustness to real-world perturbations is critical for deployment. Existing methods target simple visual disturbances, overlooking the broader multi-modal perturbations that arise in actions, instructions, environments, and observations. Here, we first evaluate the robustness of mainstream VLAs under 17 perturbations across four modalities. We find (1) actions as the most fragile modality, (2) Existing visual-robust VLA do not gain robustness in other modality, and (3) pi0 demonstrates superior robustness. To build multi-modal robust VLAs, we propose RobustVLA against perturbations in VLA inputs and outputs. For output robustness, we perform offline robust optimization against worst-case action noise that maximizes mismatch in flow matching objective. This can be seen as adversarial training, label smoothing, and outlier penalization. For input robustness, we enforce consistent actions across input variations that preserve task semantics. To account for multiple perturbations, we formulate robustness as a multi-armed bandit problem and apply an upper confidence bound algorithm to automatically identify the most harmful noise. Experiments on LIBERO demonstrate our RobustVLA delivers absolute gains over baselines of 12.6% on the pi0 backbone and 10.4% on the OpenVLA backbone across all 17 perturbations, achieving 50.6x faster inference than existing visual-robust BYOVLA that requires external LLMs, and a 10.4% gain under mixed perturbations. On the real-world FR5 robot, under four types of multimodal perturbations, RobustVLA shows strong low-data performance, outperforming pi0 by 65.6% success rate with 25 demonstrations. Even with abundant demos, our method still outperform pi0 by 30% success rate. Code and demo videos available at https://github.com/gakakulicc/RobustVLA."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Published as a conference paper at ICLR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00037","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":6,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T01:49:53Z","registered":"2025-10-02T01:49:54Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-28T03:48:11Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00180","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00180","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00180","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Amit","familyName":"Milstein","name":"Milstein, Amit","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nir","familyName":"Shlezinger","name":"Shlezinger, Nir","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Boaz","familyName":"Rafaely","name":"Rafaely, Boaz","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"DiffAU: Diffusion-Based Ambisonics Upscaling"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Audio and Speech Processing (eess.AS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Sound (cs.SD)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Signal Processing (eess.SP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-30T19:01:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:05:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-27T18:14:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-31T00:06:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-30T01:56:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06-02T00:23:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-07-14T12:05:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-07-15T00:46:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Spatial audio enhances immersion by reproducing 3D sound fields, with Ambisonics offering a scalable format for this purpose. While first-order Ambisonics (FOA) notably facilitates hardware-efficient acquisition and storage of sound fields as compared to high-order Ambisonics (HOA), its low spatial resolution limits realism, highlighting the need for Ambisonics upscaling (AU) as an approach for increasing the order of Ambisonics signals. In this work we propose DiffAU, a cascaded AU method that leverages recent developments in diffusion models combined with novel adaptation to spatial audio to generate 3rd order Ambisonics from FOA. By learning data distributions, DiffAU provides a principled approach that rapidly and reliably reproduces HOA in various settings. Experiments in anechoic conditions with multiple speakers, show strong objective and perceptual performance."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00180","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T01:53:25Z","registered":"2025-10-02T01:53:26Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-15T02:16:03Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00458","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00458","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00458","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Atif","familyName":"Belal","name":"Belal, Atif","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Heitor R.","familyName":"Medeiros","name":"Medeiros, Heitor R.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marco","familyName":"Pedersoli","name":"Pedersoli, Marco","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eric","familyName":"Granger","name":"Granger, Eric","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"VLOD-TTA: Test-Time Adaptation of Vision-Language Object Detectors"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T03:17:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:24:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-17T14:58:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-18T01:11:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-27T16:54:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06-30T00:41:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-07-02T20:01:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-07-07T00:07:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Vision-language object detectors (VLODs) such as YOLO-World and Grounding DINO exhibit strong zero-shot generalization, but their performance degrades under distribution shift. Test-time adaptation (TTA) offers a practical way to adapt models during inference using only unlabeled target (test) data. However, while TTA has made substantial progress in vision-language classification, its application to VLODs remains largely unexplored. The only prior method relies on a mean-teacher framework that introduces significant latency and memory overhead. To this end, we introduce VLOD-TTA, a TTA method that leverages dense proposal overlap and image-conditioned prompts to adapt VLODs with low additional overhead. VLOD-TTA combines (i) an IoU-weighted entropy objective that emphasizes spatially coherent proposal clusters and mitigates confirmation bias from isolated boxes, and (ii) image-conditioned prompt selection that ranks prompts by image-level compatibility and aggregates the most informative prompt scores for detection. Our experiments across diverse distribution shifts, including artistic domains, adverse driving conditions, low-light imagery, and common corruptions, indicate that VLOD-TTA consistently outperforms standard TTA baselines and the prior state-of-the-art method using YOLO-World and Grounding DINO. Our code: https://github.com/imatif17/VLOD-TTA"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00458","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T02:00:11Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:00:11Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-07T04:58:01Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01009","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01009","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01009","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ashim","familyName":"Dahal","name":"Dahal, Ashim","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ankit","familyName":"Ghimire","name":"Ghimire, Ankit","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Saydul Akbar","familyName":"Murad","name":"Murad, Saydul Akbar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nick","familyName":"Rahimi","name":"Rahimi, Nick","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"POVQA: Preference-Optimized Video Question Answering with Rationales for Data Efficiency"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multimedia (cs.MM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T15:15:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T01:01:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-24T16:54:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-25T01:14:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-30T19:53:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06-02T01:22:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Long-video multimodal question answering requires structured reasoning over visual evidence and dialogue, but Large Vision-Language Models (LVLMs) are constrained by context-window and compute limits. We propose POVQA, which compresses each second into a temporally pooled image (1 fps pooled images) to maintain dense temporal coverage under a fixed token budget. We then train Qwen2.5-VL-7B with supervised fine-tuning (SFT) on rationale+answer targets, and optionally apply Direct Preference Optimization (DPO) for preference alignment. We introduce ReasonVQA as a pilot diagnostic dataset with 12 movies and 239 human-annotated QA+rationale triplets for controlled analysis of long-context multimodal reasoning under compression. On ReasonVQA, SFT improves the best pooled-only baseline from 0.212 to 0.550 F1, showing that pooled evidence plus rationale supervision provides the main performance gains in this setting. In zero-shot transfer, POVQA also reaches 64.7\\% on TVQA after SFT+DPO. These results are preliminary: ReasonVQA is small, pooling can lose fine-grained temporal order, and DPO effects are not uniformly positive across settings. Code, dataset, and additional qualitative evaluations are available at \\href{https://povqa.github.io}{https://povqa.github.io}."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted in MAR at CVPR Workshop (Proceedings Track)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01009","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T02:14:20Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:14:21Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-02T04:53:11Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01184","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01184","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01184","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanbo","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Yanbo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sungjae","familyName":"Park","name":"Park, Sungjae","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhizhuo","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Zhizhuo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shubham","familyName":"Tulsiani","name":"Tulsiani, Shubham","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Temporal Score Rescaling for Temperature Sampling in Diffusion and Flow Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T17:59:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T01:11:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-22T21:36:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-26T00:12:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We present a mechanism to steer the sampling diversity of denoising diffusion and flow matching models, allowing users to sample from a sharper or broader distribution than the training distribution. We build on the observation that these models leverage (learned) score functions of noisy data distributions for sampling and show that rescaling these allows one to effectively control a 'local' sampling temperature. Notably, this approach does not require any finetuning or alterations to training strategy, and can be applied to any off-the-shelf model and is compatible with both deterministic and stochastic samplers. We first validate our framework on toy 2D data, and then demonstrate its application for diffusion models trained across five disparate tasks -- image generation, pose estimation, depth prediction, robot manipulation, and protein design. We find that across these tasks, our approach allows sampling from sharper (or flatter) distributions, yielding performance gains e.g., depth prediction models benefit from sampling more likely depth estimates, whereas image generation models perform better when sampling a slightly flatter distribution."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at ICML 2026. Project page: https://temporalscorerescaling.github.io/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01184","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T02:18:41Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:18:42Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-26T04:04:21Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00600","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00600","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00600","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pietro","familyName":"Mazzaglia","name":"Mazzaglia, Pietro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cansu","familyName":"Sancaktar","name":"Sancaktar, Cansu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Markus","familyName":"Peschl","name":"Peschl, Markus","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daniel","familyName":"Dijkman","name":"Dijkman, Daniel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Hybrid Training for Vision-Language-Action Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T07:27:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:35:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-19T06:34:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-20T00:38:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Using Large Language Models to produce intermediate thoughts, a.k.a. Chain-of-thought (CoT), before providing an answer has been a successful recipe for solving complex language tasks. In robotics, similar embodied CoT strategies, generating thoughts before actions, have also been shown to lead to improved performance when using Vision-Language-Action models (VLAs). As these techniques increase the length of the model's generated outputs to include the thoughts, the inference time is negatively affected. Delaying an agent's actions in real-world executions, as in robotic manipulation settings, strongly affects the usability of a method, as tasks require long sequences of actions. However, is the generation of long chains-of-thought a strong prerequisite for achieving performance improvements? In this work, we explore the idea of Hybrid Training (HyT), a framework that enables VLAs to learn from thoughts and benefit from the associated performance gains, while enabling the possibility to leave out CoT generation during inference. Furthermore, by learning to conditionally predict a diverse set of outputs, HyT supports flexibility at inference time, enabling the model to either predict actions directly, generate thoughts or follow instructions. We evaluate the proposed method in a series of simulated benchmarks and real-world experiments."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Published as a conference paper at ICLR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00600","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T02:03:50Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:03:51Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-20T02:45:49Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01172","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01172","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01172","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qingyuan","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Qingyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jia-Chen","familyName":"Gu","name":"Gu, Jia-Chen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yunzhi","familyName":"Yao","name":"Yao, Yunzhi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hong","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Hong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nanyun","familyName":"Peng","name":"Peng, Nanyun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Energy-Regularized Sequential Model Editing on Hyperspheres"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T17:55:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T01:11:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-02T09:55:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-03T02:54:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-14T05:50:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-05-15T00:32:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) require constant updates to remain aligned with evolving real-world knowledge. Model editing offers a lightweight alternative to retraining, but sequential editing often destabilizes representations and induces catastrophic forgetting. In this work, we seek to better understand and mitigate performance degradation caused by sequential editing. We hypothesize that hyperspherical uniformity, a property that maintains uniform distribution of neuron weights on a hypersphere, helps the model remain stable, retain prior knowledge, while still accommodate new updates. We use Hyperspherical Energy (HE) to quantify neuron uniformity during editing, and examine its correlation with editing performance. Empirical studies across widely used editing methods reveals a strong correlation between HE dynamics and editing performance, with editing failures consistently coinciding with high HE fluctuations. We further theoretically prove that HE dynamics impose a lower bound on the degradation of pretrained knowledge, highlighting why HE stability is crucial for knowledge retention. Motivated by these insights, we propose SPHERE (Sparse Projection for Hyperspherical Energy-Regularized Editing), an HE-driven regularization strategy that stabilizes neuron weight distributions, ultimately preserving prior knowledge while enabling reliable sequential updates. Specifically, SPHERE identifies a sparse space complementary to the principal hyperspherical directions of the pretrained weight matrices and projects new knowledge onto it, attenuating perturbations on the principal directions. Extensive experiments on LLaMA3 (8B) and Qwen2.5 (7B) show that SPHERE outperforms the best baseline in editing capability by an average of 16.41%, while most faithfully preserving general model performance, thereby offering a principled path toward reliable large-scale knowledge editing."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by ICLR 2026. The code is available at https://github.com/PlusLabNLP/SPHERE. Project page: https://www.qingyuanliu.net/sphere_projectpage/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01172","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T02:18:23Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:18:24Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-15T02:35:44Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00072","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00072","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00072","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenhui","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Chenhui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fuxun","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Fuxun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michael J.","familyName":"Bianco","name":"Bianco, Michael J.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jacob","familyName":"Kovarskiy","name":"Kovarskiy, Jacob","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Raphael","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Raphael","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qi","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Qi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zirui","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Zirui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Will","familyName":"LeVine","name":"LeVine, Will","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Brandon","familyName":"Dubbs","name":"Dubbs, Brandon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Heming","familyName":"Liao","name":"Liao, Heming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cassandra","familyName":"Burgess","name":"Burgess, Cassandra","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Suvam","familyName":"Bag","name":"Bag, Suvam","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jay","familyName":"Patravali","name":"Patravali, Jay","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rupanjali","familyName":"Kukal","name":"Kukal, Rupanjali","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mikael","familyName":"Figueroa","name":"Figueroa, Mikael","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rishi","familyName":"Madhok","name":"Madhok, Rishi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nikolaos","familyName":"Karianakis","name":"Karianakis, Nikolaos","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinjun","familyName":"Xiong","name":"Xiong, Jinjun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Unlocking Zero-Shot Geospatial Reasoning via Indirect Rewards"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-29T21:34:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:01:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-30T21:51:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-04T00:12:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Training robust reasoning vision-language models (VLMs) in rare domains (such as geospatial) is fundamentally constrained by supervision scarcity. While raw geospatial imagery is abundant, the amount of task-direct supervision falls far behind that of common domains. In this work, we validate an important conclusion: indirect verifiable rewards, derived from seemingly unrelated metadata, are sufficient to induce sophisticated and generalizable geospatial reasoning across a wide range of downstream tasks (25+). We present Geo-R1 as one empirical instantiation of this paradigm. Rather than relying on limited task-specific annotations (i.e., direct rewards), Geo-R1 utilizes scalable, verifiable indirect proxy rewards based on cross-view alignment with metadata (geolocation information) to drive reinforcement learning at scale. Such indirect rewards successfully motivate the model to discover and internalize zero-shot geospatial reasoning across diverse tasks, achieving extraordinary zero-shot transfer on out-of-distribution benchmarks and even surpassing fully supervised specialists on certain benchmarks. These findings indicate that optimizing for indirect verifiable rewards may provide a scalable pathway to unlock generalized reasoning capabilities in rare domains with massive unlabeled data archives. Our code is availavle at: https://github.com/miniHuiHui/Geo-R1."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ICML 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00072","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T01:50:46Z","registered":"2025-10-02T01:50:46Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-04T01:54:37Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00695","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00695","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00695","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Myungkyu","familyName":"Koo","name":"Koo, Myungkyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daewon","familyName":"Choi","name":"Choi, Daewon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Taeyoung","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Taeyoung","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kyungmin","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Kyungmin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Changyeon","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Changyeon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Younggyo","familyName":"Seo","name":"Seo, Younggyo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinwoo","familyName":"Shin","name":"Shin, Jinwoo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"HAMLET: Switch your Vision-Language-Action Model into a History-Aware Policy"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T09:15:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:41:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T06:41:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-03T00:28:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-15T17:01:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-04-16T01:01:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Inherently, robotic manipulation tasks are history-dependent: leveraging past context could be beneficial. However, most existing Vision-Language-Action models (VLAs) have been designed without considering this aspect, i.e., they rely solely on the current observation, ignoring preceding context. In this paper, we propose HAMLET, a scalable framework to adapt VLAs to attend to the historical context during action prediction. Specifically, we introduce moment tokens that compactly encode perceptual information at each timestep. Their representations are initialized with time-contrastive learning, allowing them to better capture temporally distinctive aspects. Next, we employ a lightweight memory module that integrates the moment tokens across past timesteps into memory features, which are then leveraged for action prediction. Through empirical evaluation, we show that HAMLET successfully transforms a state-of-the-art VLA into a history-aware policy, especially demonstrating significant improvements on long-horizon tasks that require historical context. In particular, on top of GR00T N1.5, HAMLET achieves an average success rate of 76.4% on history-dependent real-world tasks, surpassing the baseline performance by 47.2%. Furthermore, HAMLET pushes prior art performance from 64.1% to 66.4% on RoboCasa Kitchen (100-demo setup) and from 95.6% to 97.7% on LIBERO, highlighting its effectiveness even under generic robot-manipulation benchmarks."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ICLR 2026. Project page: https://myungkyukoo.github.io/hamlet/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00695","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T02:06:31Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:06:31Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-16T02:18:34Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00919","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00919","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00919","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shunfeng","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Shunfeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yudi","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yudi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Meng","familyName":"Fang","name":"Fang, Meng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zihan","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Zihan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhitan","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Zhitan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mykola","familyName":"Pechenizkiy","name":"Pechenizkiy, Mykola","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ling","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Ling","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Benchmarking Foundation Models with Retrieval-Augmented Generation in Olympic-Level Physics Problem Solving"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T13:57:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:56:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T09:55:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-03T00:37:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-14T02:32:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-04-15T00:20:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) with foundation models has achieved strong performance across diverse tasks, but their capacity for expert-level reasoning-such as solving Olympiad-level physics problems-remains largely unexplored. Inspired by the way students prepare for competitions by reviewing past problems, we investigate the potential of RAG to enhance physics reasoning in foundation models. We introduce PhoPile, a high-quality multimodal dataset specifically designed for Olympiad-level physics, enabling systematic study of retrieval-based reasoning. PhoPile includes diagrams, graphs, and equations, capturing the inherently multimodal nature of physics problem solving. Using PhoPile, we benchmark RAG-augmented foundation models, covering both large language models (LLMs) and large multimodal models (LMMs) with multiple retrievers. Our results demonstrate that integrating retrieval with physics corpora can improve model performance, while also highlighting challenges that motivate further research in retrieval-augmented physics reasoning."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to EMNLP 2025 (Findings)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00919","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T02:12:05Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:12:06Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-15T02:24:03Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00635","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00635","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00635","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nanxiang","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Nanxiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhaoxin","familyName":"Fan","name":"Fan, Zhaoxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Enhan","familyName":"Kang","name":"Kang, Enhan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daiheng","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Daiheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yun","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Yun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanxia","familyName":"Chang","name":"Chang, Yanxia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zheng","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Zheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yeying","familyName":"Jin","name":"Jin, Yeying","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenjun","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Wenjun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Erased, But Not Forgotten: Erased Rectified Flow Transformers Still Remain Unsafe Under Concept Attack"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T08:12:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:37:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-04T10:33:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:31:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-24T08:16:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:31:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-04-01T08:20:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-04-02T00:36:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-04-08T03:02:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2026-04-09T00:21:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"5","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent advances in text-to-image (T2I) diffusion models have enabled impressive generative capabilities, but they also raise significant safety concerns due to the potential to produce harmful or undesirable content. While concept erasure has been explored as a mitigation strategy, most existing approaches and corresponding attack evaluations are tailored to Stable Diffusion (SD) and exhibit limited effectiveness when transferred to next-generation rectified flow transformers such as Flux. In this work, we present ReFlux, the first concept attack method specifically designed to assess the robustness of concept erasure in the latest rectified flow-based T2I framework. Our approach is motivated by the observation that existing concept erasure techniques, when applied to Flux, fundamentally rely on a phenomenon known as attention localization. Building on this insight, we propose a simple yet effective attack strategy that specifically targets this property. At its core, a reverse-attention optimization strategy is introduced to effectively reactivate suppressed signals while stabilizing attention. This is further reinforced by a velocity-guided dynamic that enhances the robustness of concept reactivation by steering the flow matching process, and a consistency-preserving objective that maintains the global layout and preserves unrelated content. Extensive experiments consistently demonstrate the effectiveness and efficiency of the proposed attack method, establishing a reliable benchmark for evaluating the robustness of concept erasure strategies in rectified flow transformers."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00635","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":4,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T02:04:41Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:04:42Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-09T02:19:02Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00766","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00766","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00766","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eunki","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Eunki","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Na Min","familyName":"An","name":"An, Na Min","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wan Ju","familyName":"Kang","name":"Kang, Wan Ju","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sangryul","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Sangryul","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"James","familyName":"Thorne","name":"Thorne, James","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hyunjung","familyName":"Shim","name":"Shim, Hyunjung","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Are Large Vision-Language Models Ready to Guide Blind and Low-Vision Individuals?"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T10:55:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:46:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-01T10:51:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-02T00:44:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Vision-Language Models (LVLMs) demonstrate a promising direction for assisting individuals with blindness or low-vision (BLV). Yet, measuring their true utility in real-world scenarios is challenging because evaluating whether their descriptions are BLV-informative requires a fundamentally different approach from assessing standard scene descriptions. While the \"VLM-as-a-metric\" or \"LVLM-as-a-judge\" paradigm has emerged, existing evaluators still fall short of capturing the unique requirements of BLV-centric evaluation, lacking at least one of the following key properties: (1) High correlation with human judgments, (2) Long instruction understanding, (3) Score generation efficiency, and (4) Multi-dimensional assessment. To this end, we propose a unified framework to bridge the gap between automated evaluation and actual BLV needs. First, we conduct an in-depth user study with BLV participants to understand and quantify their navigational preferences, curating VL-GUIDEDATA, a large-scale BLV user-simulated preference dataset containing image-request-response-score pairs. We then leverage the dataset to develop an accessibility-aware evaluator, VL-GUIDE-S, which outperforms existing (L)VLM judges in both human alignment and inference efficiency. Notably, its effectiveness extends beyond a single domain, demonstrating strong performance across multiple fine-grained, BLV-critical dimensions. We hope our work lays as a foundation for automatic AI judges that advance safe, barrier-free navigation for BLV users."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"42 pages, 14 figures, 28 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00766","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T02:08:20Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:08:21Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-02T02:28:05Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00430","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00430","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00430","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Suhyeon","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Suhyeon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jong Chul","familyName":"Ye","name":"Ye, Jong Chul","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"PromptLoop: Plug-and-Play Prompt Refinement via Latent Feedback for Diffusion Model Alignment"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T02:18:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:21:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-25T08:43:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-26T00:38:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Despite recent progress, reinforcement learning (RL)-based fine-tuning of diffusion models often struggles with generalization, composability, and robustness against reward hacking. Recent studies have explored prompt refinement as a modular alternative, but most adopt a feed-forward approach that applies a single refined prompt throughout the entire sampling trajectory, thereby failing to fully leverage the sequential nature of reinforcement learning. To address this, we introduce PromptLoop, a plug-and-play RL framework that incorporates latent feedback into step-wise prompt refinement. Rather than modifying diffusion model weights, a multimodal large language model (MLLM) is trained with RL to iteratively update prompts based on intermediate latent states of diffusion models. This design achieves a structural analogy to the Diffusion RL approach, while retaining the flexibility and generality of prompt-based alignment. Extensive experiments across diverse reward functions and diffusion backbones demonstrate that PromptLoop (i) achieves effective reward optimization, (ii) generalizes seamlessly to unseen models, (iii) composes orthogonally with existing alignment methods, and (iv) mitigates over-optimization and reward hacking while introducing only a practically negligible inference overhead."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"CVPR26 poster. 25 pages, 19 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00430","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T01:59:29Z","registered":"2025-10-02T01:59:30Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-26T02:29:38Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00681","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00681","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00681","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinchang","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Jinchang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zijun","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Zijun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiakai","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Jiakai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guoyu","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Guoyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Adaptive Event Stream Slicing for Open-Vocabulary Event-Based Object Detection via Vision-Language Knowledge Distillation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T09:03:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:40:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-11T02:15:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-12T00:20:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Event cameras offer advantages in object detection tasks due to high-speed response, low latency, and robustness to motion blur. However, event cameras lack texture and color information, making open-vocabulary detection particularly challenging. Current event-based detection methods are typically trained on predefined categories, limiting their ability to generalize to novel objects, where encountering previously unseen objects is common. Vision-language models (VLMs) have enabled open-vocabulary object detection in RGB images. However, the modality gap between images and event streams makes it ineffective to directly transfer CLIP to event data, as CLIP was not designed for event streams. To bridge this gap, we propose an event-image knowledge distillation framework that leverages CLIP's semantic understanding to achieve open-vocabulary object detection on event data. Instead of training CLIP directly on event streams, we use image frames as inputs to a teacher model, guiding the event-based student model to learn CLIP's rich visual representations. Through spatial attention-based distillation, the student network learns meaningful visual features directly from raw event inputs while inheriting CLIP's broad visual knowledge. Furthermore, to prevent information loss due to event data segmentation, we design a hybrid spiking neural network (SNN) and convolutional neural network (CNN) framework. Unlike fixed-group event segmentation methods, which often discard crucial temporal information, our SNN adaptively determines the optimal event segmentation moments, ensuring that key temporal features are extracted. The extracted event features are then processed by CNNs for object detection."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00681","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T02:06:03Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:06:04Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-12T02:25:24Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01068","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01068","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01068","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiahang","familyName":"Cao","name":"Cao, Jiahang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yize","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Yize","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hanzhong","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Hanzhong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rui","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Rui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mu","familyName":"Nan","name":"Nan, Mu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weijian","familyName":"Mai","name":"Mai, Weijian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaxu","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Jiaxu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao","familyName":"Cheng","name":"Cheng, Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingkai","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Jingkai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gang","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Gang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wen","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Wen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qiang","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Qiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yijie","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Yijie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qihao","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Qihao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chunfeng","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Chunfeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiao","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Xiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ping","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Ping","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andrew F.","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Andrew F.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Compose Your Policies! Improving Diffusion-based or Flow-based Robot Policies via Test-time Distribution-level Composition"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T16:05:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T01:05:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-10T10:57:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-11T00:51:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Diffusion-based models for robotic control, including vision-language-action (VLA) and vision-action (VA) policies, have demonstrated significant capabilities. Yet their advancement is constrained by the high cost of acquiring large-scale interaction datasets. This work introduces an alternative paradigm for enhancing policy performance without additional model training. Perhaps surprisingly, we demonstrate that the composed policies can exceed the performance of either parent policy. Our contribution is threefold. First, we establish a theoretical foundation showing that the convex composition of distributional scores from multiple diffusion models can yield a superior one-step functional objective compared to any individual score. A Grönwall-type bound is then used to show that this single-step improvement propagates through entire generation trajectories, leading to systemic performance gains. Second, motivated by these results, we propose General Policy Composition (GPC), a training-free method that enhances performance by combining the distributional scores of multiple pre-trained policies via a convex combination and test-time search. GPC is versatile, allowing for the plug-and-play composition of heterogeneous policies, including VA and VLA models, as well as those based on diffusion or flow-matching, irrespective of their input visual modalities. Third, we provide extensive empirical validation. Experiments on Robomimic, PushT, and RoboTwin benchmarks, alongside real-world robotic evaluations, confirm that GPC consistently improves performance and adaptability across a diverse set of tasks. Further analysis of alternative composition operators and weighting strategies offers insights into the mechanisms underlying the success of GPC. These results establish GPC as a simple yet effective method for improving control performance by leveraging existing policies."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to ICLR 2026. Project Page: https://sagecao1125.github.io/GPC-Site/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01068","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T02:15:49Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:15:49Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-11T02:33:48Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00502","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00502","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00502","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jaewoo","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Jaewoo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Minsu","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Minsu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sanghyeok","familyName":"Choi","name":"Choi, Sanghyeok","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Inhyuck","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Inhyuck","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sujin","familyName":"Yun","name":"Yun, Sujin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hyeongyu","familyName":"Kang","name":"Kang, Hyeongyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Woocheol","familyName":"Shin","name":"Shin, Woocheol","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Taeyoung","familyName":"Yun","name":"Yun, Taeyoung","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kiyoung","familyName":"Om","name":"Om, Kiyoung","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinkyoo","familyName":"Park","name":"Park, Jinkyoo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Diffusion Alignment as Variational Expectation-Maximization"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T04:34:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:27:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-23T08:58:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-24T02:16:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-06T06:07:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-03-09T00:26:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Diffusion alignment aims to optimize diffusion models for the downstream objective. While existing methods based on reinforcement learning or direct backpropagation achieve considerable success in maximizing rewards, they often suffer from reward over-optimization and mode collapse. We introduce Diffusion Alignment as Variational Expectation-Maximization (DAV), a framework that formulates diffusion alignment as an iterative process alternating between two complementary phases: the E-step and the M-step. In the E-step, we employ test-time search to generate diverse and reward-aligned samples. In the M-step, we refine the diffusion model using samples discovered by the E-step. We demonstrate that DAV can optimize reward while preserving diversity for both continuous and discrete tasks: text-to-image synthesis and DNA sequence design. Our code is available at https://github.com/Jaewoopudding/dav."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ICLR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00502","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T02:01:20Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:01:21Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-09T02:08:54Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00060","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00060","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00060","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sheng","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Sheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tong","familyName":"Zhan","name":"Zhan, Tong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guancheng","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Guancheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanfeng","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Yanfeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jian","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Jian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Less is More: Lean yet Powerful Vision-Language Model for Autonomous Driving"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-29T05:14:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:01:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T15:30:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-06T00:46:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-27T09:00:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-03-02T01:34:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In this work, we reconceptualize autonomous driving as a generalized language problem and formulate the trajectory planning task as next waypoint prediction. We introduce Max-V1, a novel framework for one-stage end-to-end autonomous driving, named in tribute to the renowned Dutch racing driver Max Verstappen. Our framework presents a single-pass generation paradigm that aligns with the inherent sequentiality of driving. This approach leverages the generative capacity of the Vision-Language Model (VLM) to enable end-to-end trajectory prediction directly from front-view camera input. The efficacy of this method is underpinned by a principled supervision strategy derived from statistical modeling. This provides a well-defined learning objective, which makes the framework highly amenable to mastering complex driving policies through imitation learning from large-scale expert demonstrations. Empirically, our method achieves state-of-the-art performance on the nuScenes dataset, delivering an overall improvement of over 30% compared to prior baselines. Furthermore, it exhibits superior generalization performance on cross-domain datasets acquired from diverse vehicles, demonstrating notable potential for cross-vehicle robustness and adaptability. With these empirical strengths, this work introduces a model that enables fundamental driving behaviors, laying the foundation for the development of more capable self-driving agents. Code will be available upon publication."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00060","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T01:50:28Z","registered":"2025-10-02T01:50:28Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-02T03:06:49Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01031","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01031","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01031","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pol","familyName":"Labarbarie","name":"Labarbarie, Pol","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vincent","familyName":"Itier","name":"Itier, Vincent","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"William","familyName":"Puech","name":"Puech, William","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Secure and reversible face anonymization with diffusion models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T15:37:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T01:03:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T12:56:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-27T01:43:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Face anonymization aims to protect sensitive identity information by altering faces while preserving visual realism and utility for downstream computer vision tasks. Current methods struggle to simultaneously ensure high image quality, strong security guarantees, and controlled reversibility for authorized identity recovery at a later time. To improve the image quality of generated anonymized faces, recent methods have adopted diffusion models. However, these new diffusion-based anonymization methods do not provide a mechanism to restrict de-anonymization to trusted parties, limiting their real-world applicability. In this paper, we present the first diffusion-based framework for secure, reversible face anonymization via secret-key conditioning. Our method injects the secret key directly into the diffusion process, enabling anonymization and authorized face reconstruction while preventing unauthorized de-anonymization. The use of deterministic forward and reverse diffusion steps guarantees exact identity recovery when the correct secret key is available. Experiments on CelebA-HQ and LFW demonstrate that our approach achieves better anonymization and de-anonymization capabilities than prior work. We also show that our method remains robust to incorrect or adversarial key de-anonymization. Our code will be made publicly available."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01031","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T02:14:53Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:14:53Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-27T03:15:14Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00523","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00523","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00523","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei-Yao","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Wei-Yao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kazuya","familyName":"Tateishi","name":"Tateishi, Kazuya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qiyu","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Qiyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shusuke","familyName":"Takahashi","name":"Takahashi, Shusuke","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuki","familyName":"Mitsufuji","name":"Mitsufuji, Yuki","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"VIRTUE: Visual-Interactive Text-Image Universal Embedder"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T05:11:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:29:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-23T02:47:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-24T02:05:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multimodal representation learning models have demonstrated successful operation across complex tasks, and the integration of vision-language models (VLMs) has further enabled embedding models with instruction-following capabilities. However, existing embedding models lack visual-interactive capabilities to specify regions of interest from users (e.g., point, bounding box, mask), which have been explored in generative models to broaden their human-interactive applicability. Equipping embedding models with visual interactions not only would unlock new applications with localized grounding of user intent, which remains unexplored, but also enable the models to learn entity-level information within images to complement their global representations for conventional embedding tasks. In this paper, we propose a novel Visual-InteRactive Text-Image Universal Embedder (VIRTUE) that extends the capabilities of the segmentation model and the vision-language model to the realm of representation learning. In VIRTUE, the segmentation model can process visual prompts that pinpoint specific regions within an image, thereby enabling the embedder to handle complex and ambiguous scenarios more precisely. To evaluate the visual-interaction ability of VIRTUE, we introduce a large-scale Segmentation-and-Scene Caption Retrieval (SCaR) benchmark comprising 1M samples that aims to retrieve the text caption by jointly considering the entity with a specific object and image scene. VIRTUE consistently achieves a state-of-the-art performance with significant improvements across 36 universal MMEB (3.1%-8.5%) and five visual-interactive SCaR (15.2%-20.3%) tasks."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ICLR 2026. 25 pages. Project page: https://sony.github.io/virtue/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00523","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T02:01:56Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:01:57Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-24T04:00:02Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00565","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00565","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00565","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shojiro","familyName":"Yamabe","name":"Yamabe, Shojiro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jun","familyName":"Sakuma","name":"Sakuma, Jun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Toward Safer Diffusion Language Models: Discovery and Mitigation of Priming Vulnerability"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T06:35:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:32:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-17T05:41:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-18T01:21:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Diffusion language models (DLMs) generate tokens in parallel through iterative denoising, which can reduce latency and enable bidirectional conditioning. However, the safety risks posed by jailbreak attacks that exploit this inference mechanism are not well understood. In this paper, we reveal that DLMs have a critical vulnerability stemming from their iterative denoising process and propose a countermeasure. Specifically, our investigation shows that if an affirmative token for a harmful query appears at an intermediate step, subsequent denoising can be steered toward a harmful response even in aligned models. As a result, simply injecting such affirmative tokens can readily bypass the safety guardrails. Furthermore, we demonstrate that the vulnerability allows existing optimization-based jailbreak attacks to succeed on DLMs. Building on this analysis, we propose a novel safety alignment method tailored to DLMs that trains models to generate safe responses from contaminated intermediate states that contain affirmative tokens. Our experiments indicate that the proposed method significantly mitigates the vulnerability with minimal impact on task performance. Furthermore, our method improves robustness against conventional jailbreak attacks. Our work underscores the need for DLM-specific safety research. Our code is available at https://github.com/mdl-lab/dlm-priming-vulnerability."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at ICLR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00565","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T02:02:58Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:02:59Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-18T02:57:06Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00666","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00666","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00666","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Leah","familyName":"Bar","name":"Bar, Leah","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liron Mor","familyName":"Yosef","name":"Yosef, Liron Mor","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shai","familyName":"Zucker","name":"Zucker, Shai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Neta","familyName":"Shoham","name":"Shoham, Neta","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Inbar","familyName":"Seroussi","name":"Seroussi, Inbar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nir","familyName":"Sochen","name":"Sochen, Nir","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Geometric Unification of Generative AI with Manifold-Probabilistic Projection Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T08:50:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:40:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-15T13:08:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-16T01:42:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Most models of generative AI for images assume that images are inherently low-dimensional objects embedded within a high-dimensional space. Additionally, it is often implicitly assumed that thematic image datasets form smooth or piecewise smooth manifolds. Common approaches overlook the geometric structure and focus solely on probabilistic methods, approximating the probability distribution through universal approximation techniques such as the kernel method. In some generative models the low dimensional nature of the data manifest itself by the introduction of a lower dimensional latent space. Yet, the probability distribution in the latent or the manifold's coordinate space is considered uninteresting and is predefined or considered uniform. In this study, we address the problem of Blind Image Denoising (BID), and to some extent, the problem of generating images from noise by unifying geometric and probabilistic perspectives. We introduce a novel framework that improves upon existing probabilistic approaches by incorporating geometric assumptions that enable the effective use of kernel-based probabilistic methods. Furthermore, the proposed framework extends prior geometric approaches by combining explicit and implicit manifold descriptions through the introduction of a distance function. The resulting framework demystifies diffusion models by interpreting them as a projection mechanism onto the manifold of ``good images''. This interpretation leads to the construction of a new deterministic model, the Manifold-Probabilistic Projection Model (MPPM), which operates in both the representation (pixel) space and the latent space. We demonstrate that the Latent MPPM (LMPPM) outperforms the Latent Diffusion Model (LDM) across various datasets, achieving superior results in terms of image restoration and generation."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00666","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T02:05:35Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:05:36Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-16T03:10:48Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00040","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00040","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00040","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junjie","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Junjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziao","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Ziao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianghong","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Jianghong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaofeng","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Xiaofeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Uncovering Intrinsic Capabilities: A Paradigm for Data Curation in Vision-Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-27T02:57:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:01:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-14T12:33:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-15T01:38:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large vision-language models (VLMs) achieve strong benchmark performance, but controlling their behavior through instruction tuning remains difficult. Reducing the budget of instruction tuning dataset often causes regressions, as heuristic strategies treat models as black boxes and overlook the latent capabilities that govern learning. We introduce Capability-Attributed Data Curation (CADC), a framework that shifts curation from task-specific heuristics to intrinsic capability analysis. CADC discovers intrinsic capabilities in an unsupervised manner from gradient-based learning trajectories, attributes training data to these capabilities via influence estimation, and curates capability-aware curricula through balanced selection and staged sequencing. This transforms black-box instruction tuning into a controllable, capability-driven process. With as little as 5% of the original data, CADC surpasses full-data training on multimodal benchmarks. These results validate intrinsic capabilities as the fundamental building blocks of model learning and establish CADC as a principle paradigm for instruction data curation."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00040","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T01:49:58Z","registered":"2025-10-02T01:49:58Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-15T03:10:07Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01149","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01149","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01149","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Paul","familyName":"Teiletche","name":"Teiletche, Paul","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Quentin","familyName":"Macé","name":"Macé, Quentin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Max","familyName":"Conti","name":"Conti, Max","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Antonio","familyName":"Loison","name":"Loison, Antonio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gautier","familyName":"Viaud","name":"Viaud, Gautier","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pierre","familyName":"Colombo","name":"Colombo, Pierre","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Manuel","familyName":"Faysse","name":"Faysse, Manuel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ModernVBERT: Towards Smaller Visual Document Retrievers"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T17:41:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T01:10:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-12-14T23:22:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-16T02:05:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-16T18:33:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-12-17T02:00:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Retrieving specific information from a large corpus of documents is a prevalent industrial use case of modern AI, notably due to the popularity of Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) systems. Although neural document retrieval models have historically operated exclusively in the text space, Visual Document Retrieval (VDR) models - large vision-language decoders repurposed as embedding models which directly work with page screenshots as inputs - are increasingly popular due to the performance and indexing latency gains they offer. In this work, we show that, while cost-efficient, this approach of repurposing generative models bottlenecks retrieval performance. Through controlled experiments, we revisit the entire training pipeline, and establish a principled recipe for improving visual document retrieval models. We notably measure the impact of attention masking, image resolution, modality alignment data regimes, and late interaction centered contrastive objectives which emerge as central performance factors. Building on these insights, we release ModernVBERT, a compact 250M-parameter vision-language encoder that outperforms recent models up to 10 times larger when fine-tuned on document retrieval tasks, enabling efficient inference on cheap CPU hardware and greatly reducing latency and costs while maintaining strong performance. Models, code and data are available at https://huggingface.co/ModernVBERT."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01149","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T02:17:49Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:17:50Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-17T03:08:55Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00940","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00940","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00940","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ding Bang","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Ding Bang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kuang Hong","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Kuang Hong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tie","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Tie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yang","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Meng Fan","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Meng Fan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhi Yan","familyName":"Jia","name":"Jia, Zhi Yan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiao Xia","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Xiao Xia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qian Jin","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Qian Jin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhi Qing","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Zhi Qing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Anisotropic linear magnetoresistance in nanoflakes of Dirac semimetal NiTe2"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Mesoscale and Nanoscale Physics (cond-mat.mes-hall)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T14:16:00Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:57:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-12-06T23:31:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-09T01:39:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This work investigates the magneto-transport properties of exfoliated NiTe2 nano-flakes with varying thicknesses and disorder levels, unveiling two distinct physical mechanisms governing the observed anisotropic linear magnetoresistance (MR). For the perpendicular magnetic field configuration, the well-defined linear MR in high fields is unambiguously attributed to a classical origin. This conclusion is supported by the proportionality between the MR slope and the carrier mobility, and between the crossover field and the inverse of mobility. In stark contrast, the linear MR under parallel magnetic fields exhibits a non-classical character. It shows a pronounced enhancement with decreasing flake thickness, which correlates with an increasing hole-to-electron concentration ratio. This distinctive thickness dependence suggests an origin in the nonlinear band effects near the Dirac point, likely driven by the shift of the Fermi level. Furthermore, the strengthening of MR anisotropic with enhanced inter-layer transport contradicts the prediction of the guiding-center diffusion model for three-dimensional systems. Our findings highlight the critical roles of band topology and structural dimensional in the anomalous magneto-transport of Dirac semi-metals."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00940","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T02:12:36Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:12:37Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-09T04:36:56Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00769","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00769","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00769","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Joana C.","familyName":"Costa","name":"Costa, Joana C.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tiago","familyName":"Roxo","name":"Roxo, Tiago","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hugo","familyName":"Proença","name":"Proença, Hugo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pedro R. M.","familyName":"Inácio","name":"Inácio, Pedro R. M.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ZQBA: Zero Query Black-box Adversarial Attack"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T11:00:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:46:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-12-05T14:53:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-08T01:40:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Current black-box adversarial attacks either require multiple queries or diffusion models to produce adversarial samples that can impair the target model performance. However, these methods require training a surrogate loss or diffusion models to produce adversarial samples, which limits their applicability in real-world settings. Thus, we propose a Zero Query Black-box Adversarial (ZQBA) attack that exploits the representations of Deep Neural Networks (DNNs) to fool other networks. Instead of requiring thousands of queries to produce deceiving adversarial samples, we use the feature maps obtained from a DNN and add them to clean images to impair the classification of a target model. The results suggest that ZQBA can transfer the adversarial samples to different models and across various datasets, namely CIFAR and Tiny ImageNet. The experiments also show that ZQBA is more effective than state-of-the-art black-box attacks with a single query, while maintaining the imperceptibility of perturbations, evaluated both quantitatively (SSIM) and qualitatively, emphasizing the vulnerabilities of employing DNNs in real-world contexts. All the source code is available at https://github.com/Joana-Cabral/ZQBA."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted in ICAART 2026 Conference"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00769","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T02:08:25Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:08:25Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-08T02:47:30Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01047","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01047","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01047","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiao","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Xiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaqi","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Jiaqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuxiang","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Shuxiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianshui","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Tianshui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liang","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Liang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guangrun","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Guangrun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"In-Situ Tweedie Discrete Diffusion Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T15:51:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T01:04:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-24T14:42:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-25T02:42:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"While diffusion models excel at generating continuous data such as images, adapting them to discrete tasks has relied on indirect approaches that either operate in continuous embedding spaces or use token masking mechanisms, both of which deviate from modeling the true discrete data distribution that can be theoretically guaranteed by Tweedie's formula. We propose in-situ Tweedie Discrete Diffusion (TDD), a framework that performs diffusion guaranteed by Tweedie's formula directly within the discrete one-hot space, hence \"in-situ.\" Unlike prior methods that diffuse continuous embeddings or mask tokens, TDD directly corrupts one-hot vectors with Gaussian noise and performs iterative denoising through a timestep-conditioned cross-entropy objective rather than mean-squared-error reconstruction. At each denoising step, the model predicts class probabilities, applies argmax to obtain discrete predictions, converts them to one-hot vectors, and feeds them into the next iteration with progressively reduced noise. This process naturally unifies discriminative classification and generative modeling under a single framework. Experiments demonstrate that TDD achieves strong performance on both image classification and text generation tasks, with extensive ablation studies confirming the effectiveness of each design component. Our work establishes a principled approach to discrete diffusion that preserves the core characteristics of diffusion models while operating natively in discrete space."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01047","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T02:15:17Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:15:18Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-11-25T04:50:07Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00411","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00411","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00411","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ran","familyName":"Tong","name":"Tong, Ran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaqi","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Jiaqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tong","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Tong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xin","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Xin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Su","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Su","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lanruo","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Lanruo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiexi","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Jiexi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Does Bigger Mean Better? Comparitive Analysis of CNNs and Biomedical Vision Language Modles in Medical Diagnosis"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T01:46:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:19:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T04:22:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-03T00:24:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-16T02:41:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-11-18T01:50:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The accurate interpretation of chest radiographs using automated methods is a critical task in medical imaging. This paper presents a comparative analysis between a supervised lightweight Convolutional Neural Network (CNN) and a state-of-the-art, zero-shot medical Vision-Language Model (VLM), BiomedCLIP, across two distinct diagnostic tasks: pneumonia detection on the PneumoniaMNIST benchmark and tuberculosis detection on the Shenzhen TB dataset. Our experiments show that supervised CNNs serve as highly competitive baselines in both cases. While the default zero-shot performance of the VLM is lower, we demonstrate that its potential can be unlocked via a simple yet crucial remedy: decision threshold calibration. By optimizing the classification threshold on a validation set, the performance of BiomedCLIP is significantly boosted across both datasets. For pneumonia detection, calibration enables the zero-shot VLM to achieve a superior F1-score of 0.8841, surpassing the supervised CNN's 0.8803. For tuberculosis detection, calibration dramatically improves the F1-score from 0.4812 to 0.7684, bringing it close to the supervised baseline's 0.7834. This work highlights a key insight: proper calibration is essential for leveraging the full diagnostic power of zero-shot VLMs, enabling them to match or even outperform efficient, task-specific supervised models."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"6pages,3 figures.Uunder review of International Conference on Artificial Intelligence, Computer, Data Sciences and Applications"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00411","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T01:59:02Z","registered":"2025-10-02T01:59:03Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-11-18T05:15:43Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00478","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00478","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00478","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jing","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Jing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wonho","familyName":"Bae","name":"Bae, Wonho","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiahong","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Jiahong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenxu","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Wenxu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junhyug","familyName":"Noh","name":"Noh, Junhyug","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Vicinity-Guided Discriminative Latent Diffusion for Privacy-Preserving Domain Adaptation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T03:58:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:25:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-11T21:35:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-14T00:47:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-11T23:33:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-11-13T01:11:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent work on latent diffusion models (LDMs) has focused almost exclusively on generative tasks, leaving their potential for discriminative transfer largely unexplored. We introduce Discriminative Vicinity Diffusion (DVD), a novel LDM-based framework for a more practical variant of source-free domain adaptation (SFDA): the source provider may share not only a pre-trained classifier but also an auxiliary latent diffusion module, trained once on the source data and never exposing raw source samples. DVD encodes each source feature's label information into its latent vicinity by fitting a Gaussian prior over its k-nearest neighbors and training the diffusion network to drift noisy samples back to label-consistent representations. During adaptation, we sample from each target feature's latent vicinity, apply the frozen diffusion module to generate source-like cues, and use a simple InfoNCE loss to align the target encoder to these cues, explicitly transferring decision boundaries without source access. Across standard SFDA benchmarks, DVD outperforms state-of-the-art methods. We further show that the same latent diffusion module enhances the source classifier's accuracy on in-domain data and boosts performance in supervised classification and domain generalization experiments. DVD thus reinterprets LDMs as practical, privacy-preserving bridges for explicit knowledge transfer, addressing a core challenge in source-free domain adaptation that prior methods have yet to solve."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"39th Conference on Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS 2025)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00478","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T02:00:42Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:00:43Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-11-13T03:09:23Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01176","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01176","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01176","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiye","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Jiye","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenghui","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Chenghui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Linh","familyName":"Tran","name":"Tran, Linh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shih-En","familyName":"Wei","name":"Wei, Shih-En","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jason","familyName":"Saragih","name":"Saragih, Jason","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alexander","familyName":"Richard","name":"Richard, Alexander","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hanbyul","familyName":"Joo","name":"Joo, Hanbyul","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shaojie","familyName":"Bai","name":"Bai, Shaojie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Audio Driven Real-Time Facial Animation for Social Telepresence"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Graphics (cs.GR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Sound (cs.SD)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T17:57:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T01:11:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-01T15:10:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-04T01:35:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1145/3757377.3763854","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We present an audio-driven real-time system for animating photorealistic 3D facial avatars with minimal latency, designed for social interactions in virtual reality for anyone. Central to our approach is an encoder model that transforms audio signals into latent facial expression sequences in real time, which are then decoded as photorealistic 3D facial avatars. Leveraging the generative capabilities of diffusion models, we capture the rich spectrum of facial expressions necessary for natural communication while achieving real-time performance (\u0026lt;15ms GPU time). Our novel architecture minimizes latency through two key innovations: an online transformer that eliminates dependency on future inputs and a distillation pipeline that accelerates iterative denoising into a single step. We further address critical design challenges in live scenarios for processing continuous audio signals frame-by-frame while maintaining consistent animation quality. The versatility of our framework extends to multimodal applications, including semantic modalities such as emotion conditions and multimodal sensors with head-mounted eye cameras on VR headsets. Experimental results demonstrate significant improvements in facial animation accuracy over existing offline state-of-the-art baselines, achieving 100 to 1000 times faster inference speed. We validate our approach through live VR demonstrations and across various scenarios such as multilingual speeches."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"SIGGRAPH Asia 2025. Project page: https://jiyewise.github.io/projects/AudioRTA"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01176","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-02T02:18:29Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:18:30Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-11-04T04:57:39Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00754","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00754","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00754","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tatsuru","familyName":"Kikuchi","name":"Kikuchi, Tatsuru","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Unified Framework for Spatial and Temporal Treatment Effect Boundaries: Theory and Identification"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Econometrics (econ.EM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"General Economics (econ.GN)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Applications (stat.AP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Methodology (stat.ME)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Economics and business","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Economics and business","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T10:43:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:45:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-11T08:00:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:04:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This paper develops a unified theoretical framework for detecting and estimating boundaries in treatment effects across both spatial and temporal dimensions. We formalize the concept of treatment effect boundaries as structural parameters characterizing regime transitions where causal effects cease to operate. Building on reaction-diffusion models of information propagation, we establish conditions under which spatial and temporal boundaries share common dynamics governed by diffusion parameters (delta, lambda), yielding the testable prediction d^*/tau^* = 3.32 lambda sqrt{delta} for standard detection thresholds. We derive formal identification results under staggered treatment adoption and develop a three-stage estimation procedure implementable with standard panel data. Monte Carlo simulations demonstrate excellent finite-sample performance, with boundary estimates achieving RMSE below 10% in realistic configurations. We apply the framework to two empirical settings: EU broadband diffusion (2006-2021) and US wildfire economic impacts (2017-2022). The broadband application reveals a scope limitation -- our framework assumes depreciation dynamics and fails when effects exhibit increasing returns through network externalities. The wildfire application provides strong validation: estimated boundaries satisfy d^* = 198 km and tau^* = 2.7 years, with the empirical ratio (72.5) exactly matching the theoretical prediction 3.32 lambda sqrt{delta} = 72.5. The framework provides practical tools for detecting when localized treatments become systemic and identifying critical thresholds for policy intervention."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"73 pages, 2 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00754","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T02:08:03Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:08:04Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T04:03:06Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00207","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00207","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00207","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yunqi","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Yunqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bing","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Bing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mahdi Boloursaz","familyName":"Mashhadi","name":"Mashhadi, Mahdi Boloursaz","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"A-Long","familyName":"Jin","name":"Jin, A-Long","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanfeng","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yanfeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pei","familyName":"Xiao","name":"Xiao, Pei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rahim","familyName":"Tafazolli","name":"Tafazolli, Rahim","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Merouane","familyName":"Debbah","name":"Debbah, Merouane","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"FlowMoE: A Scalable Pipeline Scheduling Framework for Distributed Mixture-of-Experts Training"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-30T19:31:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:07:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T15:54:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:56:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The parameter size of modern large language models (LLMs) can be scaled up via the sparsely-activated Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) technique to avoid excessive increase of the computational costs. To further improve training efficiency, pipelining computation and communication has become a promising solution for distributed MoE training. However, existing work primarily focuses on scheduling tasks within the MoE layer, such as expert computing and all-to-all (A2A) communication, while neglecting other key operations including multi-head attention (MHA) computing, gating, and all-reduce communication. In this paper, we propose FlowMoE, a scalable framework for scheduling multi-type task pipelines. First, FlowMoE constructs a unified pipeline to consistently scheduling MHA computing, gating, expert computing, and A2A communication. Second, FlowMoE introduces a tensor chunk-based priority scheduling mechanism to overlap the all-reduce communication with all computing tasks. We implement FlowMoE as an adaptive and generic framework atop PyTorch. Extensive experiments with 675 typical MoE layers and four real-world MoE models across two GPU clusters demonstrate that our proposed FlowMoE framework outperforms state-of-the-art MoE training frameworks, reducing training time by 13%-57%, energy consumption by 10%-39%, and memory usage by 7%-32%."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at NeurIPS 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00207","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T01:54:05Z","registered":"2025-10-02T01:54:06Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T02:26:18Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00055","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00055","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00055","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kiran","familyName":"Nijjer","name":"Nijjer, Kiran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ryan","familyName":"Bui","name":"Bui, Ryan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Derek","familyName":"Jiu","name":"Jiu, Derek","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Adnan","familyName":"Ahmed","name":"Ahmed, Adnan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peter","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Peter","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kevin","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Kevin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lilly","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Lilly","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Adapting Large Language Models to Mitigate Skin Tone Biases in Clinical Dermatology Tasks: A Mixed-Methods Study"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Image and Video Processing (eess.IV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computers and Society (cs.CY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-28T09:40:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:01:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T09:41:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:37:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"SkinGPT-4, a large vision-language model, leverages annotated skin disease images to augment clinical workflows in underserved communities. However, its training dataset predominantly represents lighter skin tones, limiting diagnostic accuracy for darker tones. Here, we evaluated performance biases in SkinGPT-4 across skin tones on common skin diseases, including eczema, allergic-contact dermatitis, and psoriasis using the open-sourced SCIN dataset. We leveraged the SkinGPT-4 backbone to develop finetuned models for custom skin disease classification tasks and explored bias mitigation strategies. Clinical evaluation by board-certified dermatologists on six relevant skin diseases from 300 SCIN cases assessed images for diagnostic accuracy, informativity, physician utility, and patient utility. Model fairness metrics, including demographic parity and equalized odds, were calculated across skin tones. SkinGPT-4 achieved an average demographic parity of 0.10 across Fitzpatrick types, with notable differences of 0.10-0.15 between lightest and darkest tones across evaluation metrics. Model hallucinations in artifacts and anatomy occurred at a rate of 17.8. Our customized models achieved average F1, precision, and AUROC of 0.75, 0.78, and 0.78 across visually similar disease pairs. Fairness analysis showed an average demographic parity of 0.75, with a maximum disparity of 0.21 across skin tones. The best model achieved parity scores of 0.83, 0.83, 0.76, 0.89, 0.90, and 0.90 for Fitzpatrick I-VI, indicating robust fairness. Large language models such as SkinGPT-4 showed weaker performance on darker tones. Model biases exist across evaluation criteria, and hallucinations may affect diagnostic efficacy. These findings demonstrate the efficacy of training accurate, fair models using existing backbones for custom skin disease classification."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to EADV (European Academy of Dermatology) and SID (Society for Investigative Dermatology)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00055","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T01:50:20Z","registered":"2025-10-02T01:50:21Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T02:24:28Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01185","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01185","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01185","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Leyla","familyName":"Mirvakhabova","name":"Mirvakhabova, Leyla","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Babak Ehteshami","familyName":"Bejnordi","name":"Bejnordi, Babak Ehteshami","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gaurav","familyName":"Kumar","name":"Kumar, Gaurav","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hanxue","familyName":"Liang","name":"Liang, Hanxue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wanru","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Wanru","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Paul","familyName":"Whatmough","name":"Whatmough, Paul","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Dirichlet-Prior Shaping: Guiding Expert Specialization in Upcycled MoEs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T17:59:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T01:11:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Upcycling pre-trained dense models into sparse Mixture-of-Experts (MoEs) efficiently increases model capacity but often suffers from poor expert specialization due to naive weight replication. Our analysis reveals that upcycled MoEs, even with conventional regularization, exhibit low-confidence, weakly differentiated routing, hindering performance. We introduce Dirichlet-Prior Shaping Loss (DPSL), a novel router regularization technique that directly shapes routing probability distributions by matching expert assignments to a target Dirichlet prior. DPSL offers fine-grained control over expert balance and specialization, and enables encoding of inductive biases such as encouraging experts to focus on specific modalities or tasks, without requiring manual intervention; notably, DPSL is a general tool applicable to any module that outputs categorical probability distributions, extending its utility beyond MoE training. Experiments on upcycled MoE vision-language models (with Qwen2, Phi3, Llama3.2 LLM backbones) show DPSL consistently outperforms upcycling strategies and regularization techniques across standard vision-language benchmarks, addressing the critical issue of poor specialization and fostering more adaptive, higher-performing models."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01185","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T02:18:43Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:18:43Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-02T02:18:43Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01183","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01183","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01183","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiahao","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Jiahao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Luoxin","familyName":"Ye","name":"Ye, Luoxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"TaiMing","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, TaiMing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junfei","familyName":"Xiao","name":"Xiao, Junfei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiahan","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Jiahan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuxiang","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Yuxiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xijun","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Xijun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rama","familyName":"Chellappa","name":"Chellappa, Rama","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cheng","familyName":"Peng","name":"Peng, Cheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alan","familyName":"Yuille","name":"Yuille, Alan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jieneng","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Jieneng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"EvoWorld: Evolving Panoramic World Generation with Explicit 3D Memory"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T17:59:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T01:11:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Humans possess a remarkable ability to mentally explore and replay 3D environments they have previously experienced. Inspired by this mental process, we present EvoWorld: a world model that bridges panoramic video generation with evolving 3D memory to enable spatially consistent long-horizon exploration. Given a single panoramic image as input, EvoWorld first generates future video frames by leveraging a video generator with fine-grained view control, then evolves the scene's 3D reconstruction using a feedforward plug-and-play transformer, and finally synthesizes futures by conditioning on geometric reprojections from this evolving explicit 3D memory. Unlike prior state-of-the-arts that synthesize videos only, our key insight lies in exploiting this evolving 3D reconstruction as explicit spatial guidance for the video generation process, projecting the reconstructed geometry onto target viewpoints to provide rich spatial cues that significantly enhance both visual realism and geometric consistency. To evaluate long-range exploration capabilities, we introduce the first comprehensive benchmark spanning synthetic outdoor environments, Habitat indoor scenes, and challenging real-world scenarios, with particular emphasis on loop-closure detection and spatial coherence over extended trajectories. Extensive experiments demonstrate that our evolving 3D memory substantially improves visual fidelity and maintains spatial scene coherence compared to existing approaches, representing a significant advance toward long-horizon spatially consistent world modeling."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Code available at: https://github.com/JiahaoPlus/EvoWorld"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01183","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T02:18:40Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:18:41Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-02T02:18:41Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01179","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01179","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01179","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhangchen","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Zhangchen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Adriana Meza","familyName":"Soria","name":"Soria, Adriana Meza","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shawn","familyName":"Tan","name":"Tan, Shawn","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anurag","familyName":"Roy","name":"Roy, Anurag","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ashish Sunil","familyName":"Agrawal","name":"Agrawal, Ashish Sunil","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Radha","familyName":"Poovendran","name":"Poovendran, Radha","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rameswar","familyName":"Panda","name":"Panda, Rameswar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"TOUCAN: Synthesizing 1.5M Tool-Agentic Data from Real-World MCP Environments"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T17:58:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T01:11:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Model (LLM) agents are rapidly emerging as powerful systems for automating tasks across domains. Yet progress in the open-source community is constrained by the lack of high quality permissively licensed tool-agentic training data. Existing datasets are often limited in diversity, realism, and complexity, particularly regarding multi-tool and multi-turn interactions. To address this gap, we introduce Toucan, the largest publicly available tool-agentic dataset to date, containing 1.5 million trajectories synthesized from nearly 500 real-world Model Context Protocols (MCPs). Unlike prior work, Toucan leverages authentic MCP environments to generate diverse, realistic, and challenging tasks with trajectories involving real tool execution. Our pipeline first produces a broad spectrum of tool-use queries using five distinct models, applies model-based quality filtering, and then generates agentic trajectories with three teacher models using two agentic frameworks. Rigorous rule-based and model-based validation ensures high-quality outputs. We also introduce three extension mechanisms to further diversify tasks and simulate multi-turn conversations. Models fine-tuned on Toucan outperform larger closed-source counterparts on the BFCL V3 benchmark and push the Pareto frontier forward on MCP-Universe Bench."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"35 pages, 13 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01179","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T02:18:34Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:18:34Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-02T02:18:34Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01174","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01174","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01174","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanzhe","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yanzhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kevin Qinghong","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Kevin Qinghong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mike Zheng","familyName":"Shou","name":"Shou, Mike Zheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Code2Video: A Code-centric Paradigm for Educational Video Generation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multimedia (cs.MM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T17:56:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T01:11:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"While recent generative models advance pixel-space video synthesis, they remain limited in producing professional educational videos, which demand disciplinary knowledge, precise visual structures, and coherent transitions, limiting their applicability in educational scenarios. Intuitively, such requirements are better addressed through the manipulation of a renderable environment, which can be explicitly controlled via logical commands (e.g., code). In this work, we propose Code2Video, a code-centric agent framework for generating educational videos via executable Python code. The framework comprises three collaborative agents: (i) Planner, which structures lecture content into temporally coherent flows and prepares corresponding visual assets; (ii) Coder, which converts structured instructions into executable Python codes while incorporating scope-guided auto-fix to enhance efficiency; and (iii) Critic, which leverages vision-language models (VLM) with visual anchor prompts to refine spatial layout and ensure clarity. To support systematic evaluation, we build MMMC, a benchmark of professionally produced, discipline-specific educational videos. We evaluate MMMC across diverse dimensions, including VLM-as-a-Judge aesthetic scores, code efficiency, and particularly, TeachQuiz, a novel end-to-end metric that quantifies how well a VLM, after unlearning, can recover knowledge by watching the generated videos. Our results demonstrate the potential of Code2Video as a scalable, interpretable, and controllable approach, achieving 40% improvement over direct code generation and producing videos comparable to human-crafted tutorials. The code and datasets are available at https://github.com/showlab/Code2Video."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Project Page: https://showlab.github.io/Code2Video/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01174","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T02:18:26Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:18:27Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-02T02:18:27Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01141","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01141","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01141","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shruthan","familyName":"Radhakrishna","name":"Radhakrishna, Shruthan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aman","familyName":"Tiwari","name":"Tiwari, Aman","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aanjaneya","familyName":"Shukla","name":"Shukla, Aanjaneya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Masoud","familyName":"Hashemi","name":"Hashemi, Masoud","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rishabh","familyName":"Maheshwary","name":"Maheshwary, Rishabh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shiva Krishna Reddy","familyName":"Malay","name":"Malay, Shiva Krishna Reddy","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jash","familyName":"Mehta","name":"Mehta, Jash","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pulkit","familyName":"Pattnaik","name":"Pattnaik, Pulkit","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Saloni","familyName":"Mittal","name":"Mittal, Saloni","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Khalil","familyName":"Slimi","name":"Slimi, Khalil","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kelechi","familyName":"Ogueji","name":"Ogueji, Kelechi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Akintunde","familyName":"Oladipo","name":"Oladipo, Akintunde","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Soham","familyName":"Parikh","name":"Parikh, Soham","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Oluwanifemi","familyName":"Bamgbose","name":"Bamgbose, Oluwanifemi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Toby","familyName":"Liang","name":"Liang, Toby","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ahmed","familyName":"Masry","name":"Masry, Ahmed","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Khyati","familyName":"Mahajan","name":"Mahajan, Khyati","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sai Rajeswar","familyName":"Mudumba","name":"Mudumba, Sai Rajeswar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vikas","familyName":"Yadav","name":"Yadav, Vikas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sathwik Tejaswi","familyName":"Madhusudhan","name":"Madhusudhan, Sathwik Tejaswi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Torsten","familyName":"Scholak","name":"Scholak, Torsten","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sagar","familyName":"Davasam","name":"Davasam, Sagar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Srinivas","familyName":"Sunkara","name":"Sunkara, Srinivas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nicholas","familyName":"Chapados","name":"Chapados, Nicholas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Apriel-1.5-15b-Thinker"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T17:29:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T01:09:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We present Apriel-1.5-15B-Thinker, a 15-billion parameter open-weights multimodal reasoning model that achieves frontier-level performance through training design rather than sheer scale. Starting from Pixtral-12B, we apply a progressive three-stage methodology: (1) depth upscaling to expand reasoning capacity without pretraining from scratch, (2) staged continual pre-training that first develops foundational text and vision understanding, then enhances visual reasoning through targeted synthetic data generation addressing spatial structure, compositional understanding, and fine-grained perception, and (3) high-quality text-only supervised fine-tuning on curated instruction-response pairs with explicit reasoning traces spanning mathematics, coding, science, and tool use. Notably, our model achieves competitive results without reinforcement learning or preference optimization, isolating the contribution of our data-centric continual pre-training approach. On the Artificial Analysis Intelligence Index, Apriel-1.5-15B-Thinker attains a score of 52, matching DeepSeek-R1-0528 despite requiring significantly fewer computational resources. Across ten image benchmarks, its performance is on average within five points of Gemini-2.5-Flash and Claude Sonnet-3.7, a key achievement for a model operating within single-GPU deployment constraints. Our results demonstrate that thoughtful mid-training 2 design can close substantial capability gaps without massive scale, making frontier-level multimodal reasoning accessible to organizations with limited infrastructure. We release the model checkpoint, all training recipes, and evaluation protocols under the MIT license to to advance open-source research."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01141","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T02:17:37Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:17:38Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-02T02:17:38Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01126","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01126","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01126","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuxiang","familyName":"Feng","name":"Feng, Yuxiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Keyang","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Keyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hassane","familyName":"Ouchouid","name":"Ouchouid, Hassane","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ashwil","familyName":"Kaniamparambil","name":"Kaniamparambil, Ashwil","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ioannis","familyName":"Souflas","name":"Souflas, Ioannis","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Panagiotis","familyName":"Angeloudis","name":"Angeloudis, Panagiotis","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Strategic Fusion of Vision Language Models: Shapley-Credited Context-Aware Dawid-Skene for Multi-Label Tasks in Autonomous Driving"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T17:14:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T01:08:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large vision-language models (VLMs) are increasingly used in autonomous-vehicle (AV) stacks, but hallucination limits their reliability in safety-critical pipelines. We present Shapley-credited Context-Aware Dawid-Skene with Agreement, a game-theoretic fusion method for multi-label understanding of ego-view dashcam video. It learns per-model, per-label, context-conditioned reliabilities from labelled history and, at inference, converts each model's report into an agreement-guardrailed log-likelihood ratio that is combined with a contextual prior and a public reputation state updated via Shapley-based team credit. The result is calibrated, thresholdable posteriors that (i) amplify agreement among reliable models, (ii) preserve uniquely correct single-model signals, and (iii) adapt to drift. To specialise general VLMs, we curate 1,000 real-world dashcam clips with structured annotations (scene description, manoeuvre recommendation, rationale) via an automatic pipeline that fuses HDD ground truth, vehicle kinematics, and YOLOv11 + BoT-SORT tracking, guided by a three-step chain-of-thought prompt; three heterogeneous VLMs are then fine-tuned with LoRA. We evaluate with Hamming distance, Micro-Macro-F1, and average per-video latency. Empirically, the proposed method achieves a 23% reduction in Hamming distance, 55% improvement in Macro-F1, and 47% improvement in Micro-F1 when comparing with the best single model, supporting VLM fusion as a calibrated, interpretable, and robust decision-support component for AV pipelines."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"8 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01126","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T02:17:15Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:17:16Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-02T02:17:16Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01010","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01010","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01010","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuxiang","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Yuxiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiang","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Jiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ze","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Ze","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ximeng","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Ximeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yang","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jialian","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Jialian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaodong","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Xiaodong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zicheng","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Zicheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Emad","familyName":"Barsoum","name":"Barsoum, Emad","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ImageDoctor: Diagnosing Text-to-Image Generation via Grounded Image Reasoning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T15:15:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T01:01:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The rapid advancement of text-to-image (T2I) models has increased the need for reliable human preference modeling, a demand further amplified by recent progress in reinforcement learning for preference alignment. However, existing approaches typically quantify the quality of a generated image using a single scalar, limiting their ability to provide comprehensive and interpretable feedback on image quality. To address this, we introduce ImageDoctor, a unified multi-aspect T2I model evaluation framework that assesses image quality across four complementary dimensions: plausibility, semantic alignment, aesthetics, and overall quality. ImageDoctor also provides pixel-level flaw indicators in the form of heatmaps, which highlight misaligned or implausible regions, and can be used as a dense reward for T2I model preference alignment. Inspired by the diagnostic process, we improve the detail sensitivity and reasoning capability of ImageDoctor by introducing a \"look-think-predict\" paradigm, where the model first localizes potential flaws, then generates reasoning, and finally concludes the evaluation with quantitative scores. Built on top of a vision-language model and trained through a combination of supervised fine-tuning and reinforcement learning, ImageDoctor demonstrates strong alignment with human preference across multiple datasets, establishing its effectiveness as an evaluation metric. Furthermore, when used as a reward model for preference tuning, ImageDoctor significantly improves generation quality -- achieving an improvement of 10% over scalar-based reward models."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01010","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T02:14:22Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:14:23Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-02T02:14:23Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01004","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01004","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01004","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qiming","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Qiming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xingjian","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Xingjian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoyu","familyName":"Cao","name":"Cao, Xiaoyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaolong","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Xiaolong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Min","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Min","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"TextCAM: Explaining Class Activation Map with Text"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T15:11:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T01:01:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Deep neural networks (DNNs) have achieved remarkable success across domains but remain difficult to interpret, limiting their trustworthiness in high-stakes applications. This paper focuses on deep vision models, for which a dominant line of explainability methods are Class Activation Mapping (CAM) and its variants working by highlighting spatial regions that drive predictions. We figure out that CAM provides little semantic insight into what attributes underlie these activations. To address this limitation, we propose TextCAM, a novel explanation framework that enriches CAM with natural languages. TextCAM combines the precise spatial localization of CAM with the semantic alignment of vision-language models (VLMs). Specifically, we derive channel-level semantic representations using CLIP embeddings and linear discriminant analysis, and aggregate them with CAM weights to produce textual descriptions of salient visual evidence. This yields explanations that jointly specify where the model attends and what visual attributes likely support its decision. We further extend TextCAM to generate feature channels into semantically coherent groups, enabling more fine-grained visual-textual explanations. Experiments on ImageNet, CLEVR, and CUB demonstrate that TextCAM produces faithful and interpretable rationales that improve human understanding, detect spurious correlations, and preserve model fidelity."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01004","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T02:14:12Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:14:13Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-02T02:14:13Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00993","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00993","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00993","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiamian","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Jiamian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziqi","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Ziqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chaithanya Kumar","familyName":"Mummadi","name":"Mummadi, Chaithanya Kumar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sohail","familyName":"Dianat","name":"Dianat, Sohail","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Majid","familyName":"Rabbani","name":"Rabbani, Majid","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Raghuveer","familyName":"Rao","name":"Rao, Raghuveer","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chen","familyName":"Qiu","name":"Qiu, Chen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiqiang","familyName":"Tao","name":"Tao, Zhiqiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Visual Self-Refinement for Autoregressive Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T15:03:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T01:00:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Autoregressive models excel in sequential modeling and have proven to be effective for vision-language data. However, the spatial nature of visual signals conflicts with the sequential dependencies of next-token prediction, leading to suboptimal results. This work proposes a plug-and-play refinement module to enhance the complex spatial correspondence modeling within the generated visual sequence. This module operates as a post-pretraining step to jointly refine all generated tokens of autoregressive model, enhancing vision-language modeling under a shared sequential prediction framework. By leveraging global context and relationship across the tokens, our method mitigates the error accumulation issue within the sequential generation. Experiments demonstrate that the proposed method improves the generation quality, enhancing the model's ability to produce semantically consistent results."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by EMNLP2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00993","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T02:13:56Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:13:57Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-02T02:13:57Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00855","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00855","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00855","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kevin","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Kevin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kuangzhi","familyName":"Ge","name":"Ge, Kuangzhi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaowei","familyName":"Chi","name":"Chi, Xiaowei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Renrui","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Renrui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shaojun","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Shaojun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhen","familyName":"Dong","name":"Dong, Zhen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sirui","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Sirui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shanghang","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Shanghang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Can World Models Benefit VLMs for World Dynamics?"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T13:07:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:52:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Trained on internet-scale video data, generative world models are increasingly recognized as powerful world simulators that can generate consistent and plausible dynamics over structure, motion, and physics. This raises a natural question: with the advent of strong video foundational models, might they supplant conventional vision encoder paradigms for general-purpose multimodal understanding? While recent studies have begun to explore the potential of world models on common vision tasks, these explorations typically lack a systematic investigation of generic, multimodal tasks. In this work, we strive to investigate the capabilities when world model priors are transferred into Vision-Language Models: we re-purpose a video diffusion model as a generative encoder to perform a single denoising step and treat the resulting latents as a set of visual embedding. We empirically investigate this class of models, which we refer to as World-Language Models (WorldLMs), and we find that generative encoders can capture latents useful for downstream understanding that show distinctions from conventional encoders. Naming our best-performing variant Dynamic Vision Aligner (DyVA), we further discover that this method significantly enhances spatial reasoning abilities and enables single-image models to perform multi-frame reasoning. Through the curation of a suite of visual reasoning tasks, we find DyVA to surpass both open-source and proprietary baselines, achieving state-of-the-art or comparable performance. We attribute these gains to WorldLM's inherited motion-consistency internalization from video pre-training. Finally, we systematically explore extensive model designs to highlight promising directions for future work. We hope our study can pave the way for a new family of VLMs that leverage priors from world models and are on a promising path towards generalist vision learners."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Project page: https://dyva-worldlm.github.io"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00855","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T02:10:33Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:10:34Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-02T02:10:34Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00820","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00820","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00820","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiangtao","familyName":"Kong","name":"Kong, Xiangtao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rongyuan","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Rongyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuaizheng","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Shuaizheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lingchen","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Lingchen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lei","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Lei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"NSARM: Next-Scale Autoregressive Modeling for Robust Real-World Image Super-Resolution"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T12:29:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:50:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Most recent real-world image super-resolution (Real-ISR) methods employ pre-trained text-to-image (T2I) diffusion models to synthesize the high-quality image either from random Gaussian noise, which yields realistic results but is slow due to iterative denoising, or directly from the input low-quality image, which is efficient but at the price of lower output quality. These approaches train ControlNet or LoRA modules while keeping the pre-trained model fixed, which often introduces over-enhanced artifacts and hallucinations, suffering from the robustness to inputs of varying degradations. Recent visual autoregressive (AR) models, such as pre-trained Infinity, can provide strong T2I generation capabilities while offering superior efficiency by using the bitwise next-scale prediction strategy. Building upon next-scale prediction, we introduce a robust Real-ISR framework, namely Next-Scale Autoregressive Modeling (NSARM). Specifically, we train NSARM in two stages: a transformation network is first trained to map the input low-quality image to preliminary scales, followed by an end-to-end full-model fine-tuning. Such a comprehensive fine-tuning enhances the robustness of NSARM in Real-ISR tasks without compromising its generative capability. Extensive quantitative and qualitative evaluations demonstrate that as a pure AR model, NSARM achieves superior visual results over existing Real-ISR methods while maintaining a fast inference speed. Most importantly, it demonstrates much higher robustness to the quality of input images, showing stronger generalization performance. Project page: https://github.com/Xiangtaokong/NSARM"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00820","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T02:09:42Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:09:42Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-02T02:09:42Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00815","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00815","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00815","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alexandre","familyName":"Galashov","name":"Galashov, Alexandre","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ashwini","familyName":"Pokle","name":"Pokle, Ashwini","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arnaud","familyName":"Doucet","name":"Doucet, Arnaud","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arthur","familyName":"Gretton","name":"Gretton, Arthur","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mauricio","familyName":"Delbracio","name":"Delbracio, Mauricio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Valentin","familyName":"De Bortoli","name":"De Bortoli, Valentin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Learn to Guide Your Diffusion Model"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (stat.ML)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T12:21:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:50:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Classifier-free guidance (CFG) is a widely used technique for improving the perceptual quality of samples from conditional diffusion models. It operates by linearly combining conditional and unconditional score estimates using a guidance weight $ω$. While a large, static weight can markedly improve visual results, this often comes at the cost of poorer distributional alignment. In order to better approximate the target conditional distribution, we instead learn guidance weights $ω_{c,(s,t)}$, which are continuous functions of the conditioning $c$, the time $t$ from which we denoise, and the time $s$ towards which we denoise. We achieve this by minimizing the distributional mismatch between noised samples from the true conditional distribution and samples from the guided diffusion process. We extend our framework to reward guided sampling, enabling the model to target distributions tilted by a reward function $R(x_0,c)$, defined on clean data and a conditioning $c$. We demonstrate the effectiveness of our methodology on low-dimensional toy examples and high-dimensional image settings, where we observe improvements in Fréchet inception distance (FID) for image generation. In text-to-image applications, we observe that employing a reward function given by the CLIP score leads to guidance weights that improve image-prompt alignment."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00815","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T02:09:34Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:09:35Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-02T02:09:35Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00806","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00806","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00806","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fan","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Fan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiyang","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Zhiyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yousong","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Yousong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xin","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Xin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinqiao","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Jinqiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"From Seeing to Predicting: A Vision-Language Framework for Trajectory Forecasting and Controlled Video Generation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T12:11:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:49:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Current video generation models produce physically inconsistent motion that violates real-world dynamics. We propose TrajVLM-Gen, a two-stage framework for physics-aware image-to-video generation. First, we employ a Vision Language Model to predict coarse-grained motion trajectories that maintain consistency with real-world physics. Second, these trajectories guide video generation through attention-based mechanisms for fine-grained motion refinement. We build a trajectory prediction dataset based on video tracking data with realistic motion patterns. Experiments on UCF-101 and MSR-VTT demonstrate that TrajVLM-Gen outperforms existing methods, achieving competitive FVD scores of 545 on UCF-101 and 539 on MSR-VTT."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00806","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T02:09:21Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:09:22Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-02T02:09:22Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00797","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00797","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00797","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruyu","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Ruyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dongxu","familyName":"Zhuang","name":"Zhuang, Dongxu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianhua","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Jianhua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arega Getaneh","familyName":"Abate","name":"Abate, Arega Getaneh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Per Sieverts","familyName":"Nielsen","name":"Nielsen, Per Sieverts","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ben","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Ben","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiufeng","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Xiufeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Solar PV Installation Potential Assessment on Building Facades Based on Vision and Language Foundation Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T11:51:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:48:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Building facades represent a significant untapped resource for solar energy generation in dense urban environments, yet assessing their photovoltaic (PV) potential remains challenging due to complex geometries and semantic com ponents. This study introduces SF-SPA (Semantic Facade Solar-PV Assessment), an automated framework that transforms street-view photographs into quantitative PV deployment assessments. The approach combines com puter vision and artificial intelligence techniques to address three key challenges: perspective distortion correction, semantic understanding of facade elements, and spatial reasoning for PV layout optimization. Our four-stage pipeline processes images through geometric rectification, zero-shot semantic segmentation, Large Language Model (LLM) guided spatial reasoning, and energy simulation. Validation across 80 buildings in four countries demonstrates ro bust performance with mean area estimation errors of 6.2% ± 2.8% compared to expert annotations. The auto mated assessment requires approximately 100 seconds per building, a substantial gain in efficiency over manual methods. Simulated energy yield predictions confirm the method's reliability and applicability for regional poten tial studies, urban energy planning, and building-integrated photovoltaic (BIPV) deployment. Code is available at: https:github.com/CodeAXu/Solar-PV-Installation"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00797","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T02:09:08Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:09:08Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-02T02:09:08Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00778","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00778","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00778","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Seunghoo","familyName":"Hong","name":"Hong, Seunghoo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Geonho","familyName":"Son","name":"Son, Geonho","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Juhun","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Juhun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Simon S.","familyName":"Woo","name":"Woo, Simon S.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"DIA: The Adversarial Exposure of Deterministic Inversion in Diffusion Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T11:20:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:47:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Diffusion models have shown to be strong representation learners, showcasing state-of-the-art performance across multiple domains. Aside from accelerated sampling, DDIM also enables the inversion of real images back to their latent codes. A direct inheriting application of this inversion operation is real image editing, where the inversion yields latent trajectories to be utilized during the synthesis of the edited image. Unfortunately, this practical tool has enabled malicious users to freely synthesize misinformative or deepfake contents with greater ease, which promotes the spread of unethical and abusive, as well as privacy-, and copyright-infringing contents. While defensive algorithms such as AdvDM and Photoguard have been shown to disrupt the diffusion process on these images, the misalignment between their objectives and the iterative denoising trajectory at test time results in weak disruptive performance.In this work, we present the DDIM Inversion Attack (DIA) that attacks the integrated DDIM trajectory path. Our results support the effective disruption, surpassing previous defensive methods across various editing methods. We believe that our frameworks and results can provide practical defense methods against the malicious use of AI for both the industry and the research community. Our code is available here: https://anonymous.4open.science/r/DIA-13419/."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ICCV2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00778","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T02:08:39Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:08:39Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-02T02:08:39Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00710","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00710","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00710","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yihong","familyName":"Du","name":"Du, Yihong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Lecture notes: Biological propagation via reaction-diffusion equations with nonlocal diffusion and free boundary"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Analysis of PDEs (math.AP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"35K57, 35R20, 35R35","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T09:40:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:43:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"These notes are based on the lectures given in a mini-course at VIASM (Vietnam Institute for Advanced Study in Mathematics) 2025 Summer School. They give a brief account of the theory (with detailed proofs) for propagation governed by a nonlocal reaction-diffusion model with free boundaries in one space dimension. The main part is concerned with a KPP reaction term, though the basic results on the existence and uniqueness of solutions as well as on the comparison principles are for more general situations. The contents are mostly taken from published recent works of the author with several collaborators, where the kernel function was assumed to be symmetric: J(x)=J(-x). When J(x) is not symmetric, significant differences may arise in the dynamics of the model, as shown in several preprints quoted in the references at the end of these notes, but many of the existing techniques can be easily extended to cover the \"weakly non-symmetric case\", and this is done here with all the necessary details."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00710","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T02:06:57Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:06:58Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-02T02:06:58Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00690","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00690","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00690","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yunhao","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yunhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziting","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Ziting","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuai","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Shuai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tao","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Tao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chao","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Chao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junjie","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Junjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jian","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Jian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peng","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Peng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bin","familyName":"Qin","name":"Qin, Bin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ACPO: Adaptive Curriculum Policy Optimization for Aligning Vision-Language Models in Complex Reasoning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T09:11:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:41:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Aligning large-scale vision-language models (VLMs) for complex reasoning via reinforcement learning is often hampered by the limitations of existing policy optimization algorithms, such as static training schedules and the rigid, uniform clipping mechanism in Proximal Policy Optimization (PPO). In this work, we introduce Adaptive Curriculum Policy Optimization (ACPO), a novel framework that addresses these challenges through a dual-component adaptive learning strategy. First, ACPO employs a dynamic curriculum that orchestrates a principled transition from a stable, near on-policy exploration phase to an efficient, off-policy exploitation phase by progressively increasing sample reuse. Second, we propose an Advantage-Aware Adaptive Clipping (AAAC) mechanism that replaces the fixed clipping hyperparameter with dynamic, sample-wise bounds modulated by the normalized advantage of each token. This allows for more granular and robust policy updates, enabling larger gradients for high-potential samples while safeguarding against destructive ones. We conduct extensive experiments on a suite of challenging multimodal reasoning benchmarks, including MathVista, LogicVista, and MMMU-Pro. Results demonstrate that ACPO consistently outperforms strong baselines such as DAPO and PAPO, achieving state-of-the-art performance, accelerated convergence, and superior training stability."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00690","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T02:06:21Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:06:22Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-02T02:06:22Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00652","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00652","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00652","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jieer","familyName":"Ouyang","name":"Ouyang, Jieer","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoneng","familyName":"Xiang","name":"Xiang, Xiaoneng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zheng","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yangkai","familyName":"Ding","name":"Ding, Yangkai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"OTTER: Open-Tagging via Text-Image Representation for Multi-modal Understanding"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T08:31:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:38:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We introduce OTTER, a unified open-set multi-label tagging framework that harmonizes the stability of a curated, predefined category set with the adaptability of user-driven open tags. OTTER is built upon a large-scale, hierarchically organized multi-modal dataset, collected from diverse online repositories and annotated through a hybrid pipeline combining automated vision-language labeling with human refinement. By leveraging a multi-head attention architecture, OTTER jointly aligns visual and textual representations with both fixed and open-set label embeddings, enabling dynamic and semantically consistent tagging. OTTER consistently outperforms competitive baselines on two benchmark datasets: it achieves an overall F1 score of 0.81 on Otter and 0.75 on Favorite, surpassing the next-best results by margins of 0.10 and 0.02, respectively. OTTER attains near-perfect performance on open-set labels, with F1 of 0.99 on Otter and 0.97 on Favorite, while maintaining competitive accuracy on predefined labels. These results demonstrate OTTER's effectiveness in bridging closed-set consistency with open-vocabulary flexibility for multi-modal tagging applications."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at ICDM 2025 BigIS Workshop"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00652","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T02:05:07Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:05:08Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-02T02:05:08Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00647","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00647","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00647","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinlan","familyName":"Fu","name":"Fu, Jinlan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shenzhen","familyName":"Huangfu","name":"Huangfu, Shenzhen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao","familyName":"Fei","name":"Fei, Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yichong","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Yichong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoyu","familyName":"Shen","name":"Shen, Xiaoyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xipeng","familyName":"Qiu","name":"Qiu, Xipeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"See-Kiong","familyName":"Ng","name":"Ng, See-Kiong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MCM-DPO: Multifaceted Cross-Modal Direct Preference Optimization for Alt-text Generation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T08:25:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:38:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The alt-text generation task produces concise, context-relevant descriptions of images, enabling blind and low-vision users to access online images. Despite the capabilities of large vision-language models, alt-text generation performance remains limited due to noisy user annotations, inconsistent standards, and MLLMs' insensitivity to contextual information. Previous efforts to fine-tune MLLMs using supervised fine-tuning (SFT) have struggled, as SFT relies on accurate target annotations, which are often flawed in user-generated alt-text. To address this, we propose Multi-faceted Cross-modal Direct Preference Optimization (MCM-DPO), which improves alt-text generation by learning to identify better options in preference pairs without requiring precise annotations. MCM-DPO optimizes preferences across single, paired, and multi-preference dimensions, covering textual, visual, and cross-modal factors. In light of the scarcity of high-quality annotated and preference-labeled datasets for alt-text, we constructed two large-scale, high-quality datasets named TAlt and PAlt, sourced from Twitter and Pinterest. These datasets include 202k annotated alt-text samples and 18k preference pairs that cover diverse preference dimensions, aiming to support further research in this domain. Experimental results show that our proposed MCM-DPO method consistently outperforms both DPO and SFT, establishing a new state of the art in alt-text generation. We release the code and data here: https://github.com/LVUGAI/MCM-DPO"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by ACM MM 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00647","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T02:04:59Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:05:00Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-02T02:05:00Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00627","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00627","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00627","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bingzhang","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Bingzhang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kehua","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Kehua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yinhai","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yinhai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Collaborative-Distilled Diffusion Models (CDDM) for Accelerated and Lightweight Trajectory Prediction"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T08:00:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:37:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Trajectory prediction is a fundamental task in Autonomous Vehicles (AVs) and Intelligent Transportation Systems (ITS), supporting efficient motion planning and real-time traffic safety management. Diffusion models have recently demonstrated strong performance in probabilistic trajectory prediction, but their large model size and slow sampling process hinder real-world deployment. This paper proposes Collaborative-Distilled Diffusion Models (CDDM), a novel method for real-time and lightweight trajectory prediction. Built upon Collaborative Progressive Distillation (CPD), CDDM progressively transfers knowledge from a high-capacity teacher diffusion model to a lightweight student model, jointly reducing both the number of sampling steps and the model size across distillation iterations. A dual-signal regularized distillation loss is further introduced to incorporate guidance from both the teacher and ground-truth data, mitigating potential overfitting and ensuring robust performance. Extensive experiments on the ETH-UCY pedestrian benchmark and the nuScenes vehicle benchmark demonstrate that CDDM achieves state-of-the-art prediction accuracy. The well-distilled CDDM retains 96.2% and 95.5% of the baseline model's ADE and FDE performance on pedestrian trajectories, while requiring only 231K parameters and 4 or 2 sampling steps, corresponding to 161x compression, 31x acceleration, and 9 ms latency. Qualitative results further show that CDDM generates diverse and accurate trajectories under dynamic agent behaviors and complex social interactions. By bridging high-performing generative models with practical deployment constraints, CDDM enables resource-efficient probabilistic prediction for AVs and ITS. Code is available at https://github.com/bingzhangw/CDDM."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00627","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T02:04:30Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:04:30Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-02T02:04:30Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00624","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00624","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00624","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mengfei","familyName":"Xia","name":"Xia, Mengfei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nan","familyName":"Xue","name":"Xue, Nan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiapeng","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Jiapeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yujun","familyName":"Shen","name":"Shen, Yujun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"UCD: Unconditional Discriminator Promotes Nash Equilibrium in GANs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T07:58:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:37:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Adversarial training turns out to be the key to one-step generation, especially for Generative Adversarial Network (GAN) and diffusion model distillation. Yet in practice, GAN training hardly converges properly and struggles in mode collapse. In this work, we quantitatively analyze the extent of Nash equilibrium in GAN training, and conclude that redundant shortcuts by inputting condition in $D$ disables meaningful knowledge extraction. We thereby propose to employ an unconditional discriminator (UCD), in which $D$ is enforced to extract more comprehensive and robust features with no condition injection. In this way, $D$ is able to leverage better knowledge to supervise $G$, which promotes Nash equilibrium in GAN literature. Theoretical guarantee on compatibility with vanilla GAN theory indicates that UCD can be implemented in a plug-in manner. Extensive experiments confirm the significant performance improvements with high efficiency. For instance, we achieved \\textbf{1.47 FID} on the ImageNet-64 dataset, surpassing StyleGAN-XL and several state-of-the-art one-step diffusion models. The code will be made publicly available."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00624","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T02:04:25Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:04:26Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-02T02:04:26Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00604","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00604","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00604","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yunbo","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Yunbo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuesong","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Xuesong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jia","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Jia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhenzhen","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Zhenzhen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Richang","familyName":"Hong","name":"Hong, Richang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Disentangling Foreground and Background for vision-Language Navigation via Online Augmentation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T07:32:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:35:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Following language instructions, vision-language navigation (VLN) agents are tasked with navigating unseen environments. While augmenting multifaceted visual representations has propelled advancements in VLN, the significance of foreground and background in visual observations remains underexplored. Intuitively, foreground regions provide semantic cues, whereas the background encompasses spatial connectivity information. Inspired on this insight, we propose a Consensus-driven Online Feature Augmentation strategy (COFA) with alternative foreground and background features to facilitate the navigable generalization. Specifically, we first leverage semantically-enhanced landmark identification to disentangle foreground and background as candidate augmented features. Subsequently, a consensus-driven online augmentation strategy encourages the agent to consolidate two-stage voting results on feature preferences according to diverse instructions and navigational locations. Experiments on REVERIE and R2R demonstrate that our online foreground-background augmentation boosts the generalization of baseline and attains state-of-the-art performance."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00604","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T02:03:56Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:03:57Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-02T02:03:57Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00603","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00603","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00603","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sheng","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Sheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuanmin","familyName":"Ning","name":"Ning, Yuanmin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bingxi","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Bingxi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peiyin","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Peiyin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhaohui","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Zhaohui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"LVLMs as inspectors: an agentic framework for category-level structural defect annotation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T07:31:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:35:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Automated structural defect annotation is essential for ensuring infrastructure safety while minimizing the high costs and inefficiencies of manual labeling. A novel agentic annotation framework, Agent-based Defect Pattern Tagger (ADPT), is introduced that integrates Large Vision-Language Models (LVLMs) with a semantic pattern matching module and an iterative self-questioning refinement mechanism. By leveraging optimized domain-specific prompting and a recursive verification process, ADPT transforms raw visual data into high-quality, semantically labeled defect datasets without any manual supervision. Experimental results demonstrate that ADPT achieves up to 98% accuracy in distinguishing defective from non-defective images, and 85%-98% annotation accuracy across four defect categories under class-balanced settings, with 80%-92% accuracy on class-imbalanced datasets. The framework offers a scalable and cost-effective solution for high-fidelity dataset construction, providing strong support for downstream tasks such as transfer learning and domain adaptation in structural damage assessment."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00603","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T02:03:55Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:03:56Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-02T02:03:56Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00483","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00483","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00483","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuheng","familyName":"Ji","name":"Ji, Yuheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huajie","familyName":"Tan","name":"Tan, Huajie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cheng","familyName":"Chi","name":"Chi, Cheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yijie","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Yijie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuting","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Yuting","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Enshen","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Enshen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huaihai","familyName":"Lyu","name":"Lyu, Huaihai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pengwei","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Pengwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhongyuan","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zhongyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shanghang","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Shanghang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaolong","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Xiaolong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MathSticks: A Benchmark for Visual Symbolic Compositional Reasoning with Matchstick Puzzles"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T04:04:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:26:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We introduce \\textsc{MathSticks}, a benchmark for Visual Symbolic Compositional Reasoning (VSCR), which unifies visual perception, symbolic manipulation, and arithmetic consistency. Each task presents an incorrect matchstick equation that must be corrected by moving one or two sticks under strict conservation rules. The benchmark includes both text-guided and purely visual settings, systematically covering digit scale, move complexity, solution multiplicity, and operator variation, with 1.4M generated instances and a curated test set. Evaluations of 14 vision--language models reveal substantial limitations: closed-source models succeed only on simple cases, open-source models fail in the visual regime, while humans exceed 90\\% accuracy. These findings establish \\textsc{MathSticks} as a rigorous testbed for advancing compositional reasoning across vision and symbols. Our code and dataset are publicly available at https://github.com/Yuheng2000/MathSticks."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00483","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T02:00:50Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:00:50Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-02T02:00:50Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00406","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00406","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00406","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hengtao","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Hengtao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pengxiang","familyName":"Ding","name":"Ding, Pengxiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Runze","familyName":"Suo","name":"Suo, Runze","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yihao","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yihao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zirui","familyName":"Ge","name":"Ge, Zirui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dongyuan","familyName":"Zang","name":"Zang, Dongyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kexian","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Kexian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingyang","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Mingyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongyin","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Hongyin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Donglin","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Donglin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weihua","familyName":"Su","name":"Su, Weihua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"VLA-RFT: Vision-Language-Action Reinforcement Fine-tuning with Verified Rewards in World Simulators"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T01:33:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:18:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Vision-Language-Action (VLA) models enable embodied decision-making but rely heavily on imitation learning, leading to compounding errors and poor robustness under distribution shift. Reinforcement learning (RL) can mitigate these issues yet typically demands costly real-world interactions or suffers from sim-to-real gaps. We introduce VLA-RFT, a reinforcement fine-tuning framework that leverages a data-driven world model as a controllable simulator. Trained from real interaction data, the simulator predicts future visual observations conditioned on actions, allowing policy rollouts with dense, trajectory-level rewards derived from goal-achieving references. This design delivers an efficient and action-aligned learning signal, drastically lowering sample requirements. With fewer than 400 fine-tuning steps, VLA-RFT surpasses strong supervised baselines and achieves greater efficiency than simulator-based RL. Moreover, it exhibits strong robustness under perturbed conditions, sustaining stable task execution. Our results establish world-model-based RFT as a practical post-training paradigm to enhance the generalization and robustness of VLA models. For more details, please refer to https://vla-rft.github.io/."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00406","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T01:58:55Z","registered":"2025-10-02T01:58:56Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-02T01:58:56Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00383","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00383","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00383","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu","familyName":"Cheng","name":"Cheng, Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianle","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Tianle","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Generative AI for subgrid turbulence in large-eddy simulations"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Fluid Dynamics (physics.flu-dyn)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Atmospheric and Oceanic Physics (physics.ao-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T00:54:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:17:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Turbulence governs the transport of momentum, energy, and scalars in many geophysical and engineering flows. In large-eddy simulations (LES), parameterizing subgrid-scale (SGS) stresses remains a central challenge, as unresolved physical processes strongly influence turbulent transport. Traditional SGS models, such as the Smagorinsky-type models and deep neural networks (DNNs), are deterministic and cannot capture the stochastic nature of turbulence. Despite its wide application in computer vision and natural language processing, generative artificial intelligence (AI) has not previously been applied to directly compute SGS stresses in three-dimensional turbulent boundary layers at high Reynolds numbers. Here we introduce a denoising diffusion probabilistic model (DDPM) to reconstruct SGS stresses from coarse-grained velocity fields in direct numerical simulations of the atmospheric boundary layer. The DDPM consistently outperforms Smagorinsky-type models and previous deep neural networks in terms of spatial correlations and probability distributions for deviatoric stresses, and can be applied to unseen convective stability conditions and resolutions. By learning conditional distributions rather than pointwise values, this generative approach opens a new direction for SGS turbulence modeling at high Reynolds numbers."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"18 pages, 4 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00383","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T01:58:22Z","registered":"2025-10-02T01:58:22Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-02T01:58:22Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00376","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00376","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00376","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arpan","familyName":"Mahara","name":"Mahara, Arpan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Md Rezaul Karim","familyName":"Khan","name":"Khan, Md Rezaul Karim","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Naphtali","familyName":"Rishe","name":"Rishe, Naphtali","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenjia","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Wenjia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Seyed Masoud","familyName":"Sadjadi","name":"Sadjadi, Seyed Masoud","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Discrete Wavelet Transform as a Facilitator for Expressive Latent Space Representation in Variational Autoencoders in Satellite Imagery"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T00:49:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:16:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Latent Diffusion Models (LDM), a subclass of diffusion models, mitigate the computational complexity of pixel-space diffusion by operating within a compressed latent space constructed by Variational Autoencoders (VAEs), demonstrating significant advantages in Remote Sensing (RS) applications. Though numerous studies enhancing LDMs have been conducted, investigations explicitly targeting improvements within the intrinsic latent space remain scarce. This paper proposes an innovative perspective, utilizing the Discrete Wavelet Transform (DWT) to enhance the VAE's latent space representation, designed for satellite imagery. The proposed method, ExpDWT-VAE, introduces dual branches: one processes spatial domain input through convolutional operations, while the other extracts and processes frequency-domain features via 2D Haar wavelet decomposition, convolutional operation, and inverse DWT reconstruction. These branches merge to create an integrated spatial-frequency representation, further refined through convolutional and diagonal Gaussian mapping into a robust latent representation. We utilize a new satellite imagery dataset housed by the TerraFly mapping system to validate our method. Experimental results across several performance metrics highlight the efficacy of the proposed method at enhancing latent space representation."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"6 pages, 3 Figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00376","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T01:58:11Z","registered":"2025-10-02T01:58:12Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-02T01:58:12Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00367","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00367","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00367","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dehao","familyName":"Dai","name":"Dai, Dehao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianqing","familyName":"Fan","name":"Fan, Jianqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yihong","familyName":"Gu","name":"Gu, Yihong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Debarghya","familyName":"Mukherjee","name":"Mukherjee, Debarghya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"CINDES: Classification induced neural density estimator and simulator"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (stat.ML)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Statistics Theory (math.ST)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Methodology (stat.ME)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"62G08","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T00:21:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:15:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Neural network-based methods for (un)conditional density estimation have recently gained substantial attention, as various neural density estimators have outperformed classical approaches in real-data experiments. Despite these empirical successes, implementation can be challenging due to the need to ensure non-negativity and unit-mass constraints, and theoretical understanding remains limited. In particular, it is unclear whether such estimators can adaptively achieve faster convergence rates when the underlying density exhibits a low-dimensional structure. This paper addresses these gaps by proposing a structure-agnostic neural density estimator that is (i) straightforward to implement and (ii) provably adaptive, attaining faster rates when the true density admits a low-dimensional composition structure. Another key contribution of our work is to show that the proposed estimator integrates naturally into generative sampling pipelines, most notably score-based diffusion models, where it achieves provably faster convergence when the underlying density is structured. We validate its performance through extensive simulations and a real-data application."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"50 pages, 1 figure"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00367","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T01:57:58Z","registered":"2025-10-02T01:57:59Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-02T01:57:59Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00351","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00351","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00351","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rohit","familyName":"Dilip","name":"Dilip, Rohit","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Evan","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Evan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ayush","familyName":"Varshney","name":"Varshney, Ayush","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David","familyName":"Van Valen","name":"Van Valen, David","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Flow Autoencoders are Effective Protein Tokenizers"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Biomolecules (q-bio.BM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Biological sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Biological sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-30T23:29:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:14:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Protein structure tokenizers enable the creation of multimodal models of protein structure, sequence, and function. Current approaches to protein structure tokenization rely on bespoke components that are invariant to spatial symmetries, but that are challenging to optimize and scale. We present Kanzi, a flow-based tokenizer for tokenization and generation of protein structures. Kanzi consists of a diffusion autoencoder trained with a flow matching loss. We show that this approach simplifies several aspects of protein structure tokenizers: frame-based representations can be replaced with global coordinates, complex losses are replaced with a single flow matching loss, and SE(3)-invariant attention operations can be replaced with standard attention. We find that these changes stabilize the training of parameter-efficient models that outperform existing tokenizers on reconstruction metrics at a fraction of the model size and training cost. An autoregressive model trained with Kanzi outperforms similar generative models that operate over tokens, although it does not yet match the performance of state-of-the-art continuous diffusion models. Code is available here: https://github.com/rdilip/kanzi/."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00351","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T01:57:35Z","registered":"2025-10-02T01:57:36Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-02T01:57:36Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00161","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00161","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00161","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kimihiro","familyName":"Hasegawa","name":"Hasegawa, Kimihiro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wiradee","familyName":"Imrattanatrai","name":"Imrattanatrai, Wiradee","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Masaki","familyName":"Asada","name":"Asada, Masaki","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ken","familyName":"Fukuda","name":"Fukuda, Ken","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Teruko","familyName":"Mitamura","name":"Mitamura, Teruko","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"TAMA: Tool-Augmented Multimodal Agent for Procedural Activity Understanding"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-30T18:34:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:04:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Procedural activity assistants potentially support humans in a variety of settings, from our daily lives, e.g., cooking or assembling flat-pack furniture, to professional situations, e.g., manufacturing or biological experiments. Despite its potential use cases, the system development tailored for such an assistant is still underexplored. In this paper, we propose a novel framework, called TAMA, a Tool-Augmented Multimodal Agent, for procedural activity understanding. TAMA enables interleaved multimodal reasoning by making use of multimedia-returning tools in a training-free setting. Our experimental result on the multimodal procedural QA dataset, ProMQA-Assembly, shows that our approach can improve the performance of vision-language models, especially GPT-5 and MiMo-VL. Furthermore, our ablation studies provide empirical support for the effectiveness of two features that characterize our framework, multimedia-returning tools and agentic flexible tool selection. We believe our proposed framework and experimental results facilitate the thinking with images paradigm for video and multimodal tasks, let alone the development of procedural activity assistants."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"21 pages. Code: https://github.com/kimihiroh/tama"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00161","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T01:52:57Z","registered":"2025-10-02T01:52:58Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-02T01:52:58Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00129","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00129","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00129","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hengkui","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Hengkui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liujiang","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Liujiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jihua","familyName":"He","name":"He, Jihua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qihao","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Qihao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Keke","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Keke","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuyang","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Shuyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Renle","familyName":"Fu","name":"Fu, Renle","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dahao","familyName":"Liang","name":"Liang, Dahao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lingyu","familyName":"Zeng","name":"Zeng, Lingyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bruce","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Bruce","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuan","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jin","familyName":"Zhan","name":"Zhan, Jin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaqiang","familyName":"Niu","name":"Niu, Jiaqiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinglong","familyName":"Jia","name":"Jia, Xinglong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yaqin","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Yaqin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenjun","familyName":"Ji","name":"Ji, Wenjun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Panpan","familyName":"Chi","name":"Chi, Panpan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ken","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Ken","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hengyuan","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Hengyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yingsi","familyName":"Xin","name":"Xin, Yingsi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yongfeng","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Yongfeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuexin","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yuexin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Manqi","familyName":"Ruan","name":"Ruan, Manqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ningtao","familyName":"Bian","name":"Bian, Ningtao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaohua","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Xiaohua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weipeng","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Weipeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"BigBang-Proton Technical Report: Next-Word-Prediction is Scientific Multitask Learner"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Materials Science (cond-mat.mtrl-sci)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computational Physics (physics.comp-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2.6; I.2.7; J.2; I.6.3; K.4.1","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"},{"subject":"68T05, 68T50, 00A69, 94A99","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-30T18:09:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:03:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We introduce BigBang-Proton, a unified sequence-based architecture for auto-regressive language modeling pretrained on cross-scale, cross-structure, cross-discipline real-world scientific tasks to construct a scientific multi-task learner. BigBang-Proton incorporates three fundamental innovations compared to mainstream general-purpose LLMs: Theory-Experiment Learning paradigm aligns large-scale numerical experimental data with theoretical text corpora; Binary Patch Encoding replaces byte pair encoding(BPE) tokenization; Monte Carlo Attention substitutes traditional transformer architectures. Through next-word-prediction pretraining on cross-discipline scientific datasets of real-world problems mixed with general textual corpus, followed by fine-tuning and inference on downstream tasks, BigBang-Proton demonstrates 100\\% accuracy in up to 50-digit arithmetic addition operations, performance on par with leading specialized models in particle physics jet tagging, matching MAE of specialized models in inter-atomic potential simulation, performance comparable to traditional spatiotemporal models in water quality prediction, and benchmark-exceeding performance in genome modeling. These results prove that language-guided scientific computing can match or exceed the performance of task-specific scientific models while maintaining multitask learning capabilities. We further hypothesize to scale the pretraining to the universe scale as a fundamental step toward developing material world foundational model."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"93 pages, 39 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00129","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T01:52:09Z","registered":"2025-10-02T01:52:10Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-02T01:52:10Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00088","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00088","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00088","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sagnik","familyName":"Basu","name":"Basu, Sagnik","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shubham","familyName":"Prakash","name":"Prakash, Shubham","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ashish Maruti","familyName":"Barge","name":"Barge, Ashish Maruti","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Siddharth D","familyName":"Jaiswal","name":"Jaiswal, Siddharth D","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Abhisek","familyName":"Dash","name":"Dash, Abhisek","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Saptarshi","familyName":"Ghosh","name":"Ghosh, Saptarshi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Animesh","familyName":"Mukherjee","name":"Mukherjee, Animesh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Judging by Appearances? Auditing and Intervening Vision-Language Models for Bail Prediction"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computers and Society (cs.CY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-30T12:11:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:02:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) have been extensively used for legal judgment prediction tasks based on case reports and crime history. However, with a surge in the availability of large vision language models (VLMs), legal judgment prediction systems can now be made to leverage the images of the criminals in addition to the textual case reports/crime history. Applications built in this way could lead to inadvertent consequences and be used with malicious intent. In this work, we run an audit to investigate the efficiency of standalone VLMs in the bail decision prediction task. We observe that the performance is poor across multiple intersectional groups and models \\textit{wrongly deny bail to deserving individuals with very high confidence}. We design different intervention algorithms by first including legal precedents through a RAG pipeline and then fine-tuning the VLMs using innovative schemes. We demonstrate that these interventions substantially improve the performance of bail prediction. Our work paves the way for the design of smarter interventions on VLMs in the future, before they can be deployed for real-world legal judgment prediction."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00088","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T01:51:09Z","registered":"2025-10-02T01:51:10Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-02T01:51:10Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00047","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00047","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00047","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sihao","familyName":"Ding","name":"Ding, Sihao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Santosh","familyName":"Vasa","name":"Vasa, Santosh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aditi","familyName":"Ramadwar","name":"Ramadwar, Aditi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Explanation-Driven Counterfactual Testing for Faithfulness in Vision-Language Model Explanations"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-27T15:16:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:01:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Vision-Language Models (VLMs) often produce fluent Natural Language Explanations (NLEs) that sound convincing but may not reflect the causal factors driving predictions. This mismatch of plausibility and faithfulness poses technical and governance risks. We introduce Explanation-Driven Counterfactual Testing (EDCT), a fully automated verification procedure for a target VLM that treats the model's own explanation as a falsifiable hypothesis. Given an image-question pair, EDCT: (1) obtains the model's answer and NLE, (2) parses the NLE into testable visual concepts, (3) generates targeted counterfactual edits via generative inpainting, and (4) computes a Counterfactual Consistency Score (CCS) using LLM-assisted analysis of changes in both answers and explanations. Across 120 curated OK-VQA examples and multiple VLMs, EDCT uncovers substantial faithfulness gaps and provides regulator-aligned audit artifacts indicating when cited concepts fail causal tests."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"NeurIPS 2025 workshop on Regulatable ML"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00047","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T01:50:08Z","registered":"2025-10-02T01:50:09Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-02T01:50:09Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}}],"meta":{"total":69,"totalPages":1,"page":1},"links":{"self":"https://api.datacite.org/dois?client-id=arxiv.content\u0026query=registered%3A%5B2025-10-01T16%3A00%3A00Z+TO+2025-10-02T15%3A59%3A59Z%5D+AND+%28%22vision+language%22+OR+%22vision-language%22+OR+%22world+model%22+OR+%22world+models%22+OR+%22multimodal+model%22+OR+%22multimodal+models%22+OR+VLA+OR+%22diffusion+model%22+OR+%22diffusion+models%22%29\u0026page%5Bsize%5D=100\u0026page%5Bnumber%5D=1\u0026sort=registered"}}